{"parentUuid":"f9b07712-abd0-483f-92d5-48876e8e9a8f","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ad490fb3266cc50f1","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffbfnidqEhi61uJoJF3J","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015JAwh8nNdvJgRwVD2bsRr6","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch5a_opus.md; echo ======; cat /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_supertraining_ch5a.txt; echo =====; cat /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch4-3-4_applied.md | head -60","description":"Read review, scope file, and example fix log"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":32144,"cache_read_input_tokens":18241,"output_tokens":261,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":23},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":32144},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":261,"cache_read_input_tokens":18241,"cache_creation_input_tokens":32144,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":32144,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_015JAwh8nNdvJgRwVD2bsRr6":{"command":"cat /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch5a_opus.md; echo ======; cat /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_supertraining_ch5a.txt; echo =====; cat /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch4-3-4_applied.md | head -60","description":"Read review, scope file, and example fix log"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffbfnT1qiaTp4aziiWAZ","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"a11950b3-02a9-4379-932d-1cc41eaeadd9","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:12:40.697Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"a11950b3-02a9-4379-932d-1cc41eaeadd9","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"06ea19f5-0c14-4f73-9ed0-7b72125de0b9","agentId":"ad490fb3266cc50f1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","content":"Exit code 1\n# Review: Supertraining Ch 5a (5.1 to 5.2.3), independent falsification pass\n\nReviewer: Claude Opus (independent of the writer). Date: 2026-10-03.\nScope: the 19 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch5a.txt`, folder `staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/`.\nSource: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx 266-278 (printed 255-267). Every page in range was rendered to `staging/_renders/r5a_p266.png` ... `r5a_p278.png` and opened as an image. Every equation, table, figure and number cited below was read from the image, not the text layer.\nBaseline: every \"Eric's baseline:\" quote checked against `docs/method-baseline.md` at the cited line (lines 61, 67, 73, 76, 78, 79, 96, 97, 101, 103, 104, 105, 107, 130, 134, 141, 181, 182, 188, 260). All quotes and line numbers match. Tension 16 is correctly named at line 260.\nLinks: every `[[wikilink]]` in the 19 files resolves to a file in the vault library or the staging library. Claims the entries make about linked entries were spot-checked (Bischke & Morehouse in the PAP entry; breath-holding in the isometric drawbacks entry; Moyer wearable-resistance entry \"wrong in three separate ways\"; Bondarchuk leg-to-leg 50 m \"all levels\"; Vasiliev uniform-gain in the threshold entry; APRE named in the isokinetic entry). All hold.\nDuplicates: searched the vault library and all staging libraries (including the 5b and 5c files in the same folder) for impulse-momentum, DeLorme, APRE/DAPRE, Berger, Ivanov 1966, Lelikov, analytical-synthetic, cross training, multi-faceted, Rogener, Egstrom, Zubov, thigh cuffs, Table 5.3, Krusen, Narikashvili, 6SM, \"20% of 1RM\", \"independent qualities\", \"literally copying\". No entry in scope restates an existing entry without a new angle. The Newton entry reuses Fig 5.1 (already atomized from Ch 2.2.2) but says so and adds the impulse-momentum angle.\n\nCounts: **GO 11, FIX 8, NO-GO 0.**\n\n## Per-entry verdicts\n\n| file | verdict | problem (entry vs source, with page) | exact correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve.md | FIX | `proven` is correctly scoped to the impulse-momentum identity, and (a), (b), (c) are correct physics. Three physics sentences are imprecise. (1) The entry quotes the book's \"I = F.ΔT = m.ΔV\" and \"the impulse may be calculated from the change in momentum, m.ΔV\" without flagging that the book defines ΔV as \"The final velocity reached at the end of this phase\" with the integral running from 0 (PDF 267 / p.256, image). m times a final velocity equals the change in momentum only if the body starts from rest. (2) When to apply: \"a driver metric such as propulsive impulse to the output metric (take-off velocity, hence jump height)\". Only the net impulse (vertical GRF minus body weight) fixes take-off velocity; GRF impulse alone does not. (3) When NOT (d): \"constant-speed circular motion has a constant-size force that only turns\". A force that turns is a changing force vector, so this does not counter the book's \"associated with a change in force\"; it confirms it. (c) also says \"net force on a free body\", which is loose. | (1) Add to When NOT as (e): \"Inference: the book defines ΔV as the final velocity of the phase and integrates from 0, so m.ΔV equals the change in momentum only when the body starts from rest; in general impulse = m(v_final - v_initial).\" (2) Replace \"propulsive impulse\" with \"net propulsive impulse (vertical ground reaction force minus body weight, integrated over the propulsive phase)\". (3) In (d), replace the circular-motion example with: \"a projectile under gravity changes direction continuously while the force on it stays constant, so a change in velocity direction does not require a change in force, only a net force.\" In (c), replace \"net force on a free body\" with \"net external force on the body\". Keep `proven` with its existing scope. |\n| principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities.md | GO | none | checked: \"totally independent qualities\", \"does not necessarily enhance\", \"(Verkhoshansky, 1963)\", \"inversely proportional to one another (see Ch 4)\" at PDF 268 / p.257 (image); Example labelled Inference; baseline line 101 quote and Explicit Q1 tag; `supported` appropriate. Suggest reciprocal link to `framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation` (5.4, transfer weakens with qualification). |\n| heuristic--analytical-synthetic-and-multifaceted-preparation-suit-only-early-training.md | GO | none | checked: analytical-synthetic definition, gymnasts/weightlifters sprints and barbell for track (Verkhoshansky 1977), \"appropriate only for the initial stages ... serious error for highly-qualified athletes to use them casually\", \"partly true and only under certain circumstances\", Yakovlev 1968 / Zimkin 1965 / Zatsiorsky 1965, \"inevitably opposes the law of gradual development\", cross training \"(see 5.5)\", \"sequentiality, simultane\n\n... [13117 characters truncated] ...\n\nmal implement, with the heavy option sized per athlete\". Keep the Bondarchuk quote but introduce it as \"Inference: Bondarchuk goes further and argues for small deviations from competition weight; ...\". Add to When NOT: \"Inference: several of the book's own examples are far from competition mass (a 3 kg javelin training weight; a 0.6-0.8 kg puck; 2 kg bats), so the page does not support a 'stay close to competition mass' rule; only the shot and baseball data fit it.\" (Competition masses are outside-book knowledge, hence the label.) |\n| case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work.md | GO | none | checked (image): \"38-40%\", 3 x 3 at 80% 1RM, 10 min rest, Fig 5.4 caption \"(Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999)\", first effort about 42-49 cm, second about 50 to 67-69 cm, levelling after about movement 8 at PDF 276 / p.265; Table 5.3 every cell (44.0/61.0/+38.8; 0.266/0.250/-6.8; 62.0/65.0/+4.8; 2.330/2.600/+11.7; 5.550/7.430/+33.8; 1.310/1.930/+47.3) and \"(Verkhoshansky, 1977)\" at PDF 277 / p.266. Recomputations confirmed: +38.6%, -6.0%, 0.62/0.266 = 2.33, 0.65/0.250 = 2.60, F x v = 12.9 and 19.3. Bischke & Morehouse 1950 present in linked PAP entry. `untested` acceptable for a single unreported-n experiment. |\n| heuristic--cyclic-vs-acyclic-speed-strength-tempo-rest-and-fatigue.md | GO | none | checked (image): rest-interval determinants, \"0.5-1 minute\", \"favourable emotional state\", cyclic relaxation phases, \"inversely proportional\", Korobkov 1953 / Monogarov 1958, Diachkov 1961 \"approach the tempo ... and even exceed it\" at PDF 277 / p.266; \"more demanding\" water resistance and limb loads at PDF 277-278; fatigue rule and \"empirical methods\" at PDF 278 / p.267. Baseline lines 96, 97. |\n| case-study--alternate-leg-bounding-with-thigh-cuffs-for-sprinters.md | GO | none | checked (image, PDF 278 / p.267): power greater than running, hip-flexion gather cue, 50-100 m, 100-150 g thigh cuffs, \"experienced athletes\", Zubov; third-person Verkhoshansky correctly attributed to Siff; `untested` right for a coach's anecdote. Bondarchuk and Moyer cross-references match the linked entries. Baseline line 97. |\n\n## Missing (important ideas in PDF 266-278 no entry captures)\n\nCoverage is near complete. Two small gaps, neither needs a new entry:\n\n1. PDF 270 / p.259: \"most weightlifting training is done with 1-3 repetitions for advanced weightlifters, thereby emphasizing the importance of 1RM, 2RM and 3RM loads ... especially where strength-skill is involved.\" Distinct from Vorobyev (1978) \"1-6\". Fold into the Berger entry (see its FIX).\n2. PDF 278 / p.267: the section's organising conclusion that perfecting speed-strength means combining two levers, \"the positive after-effect of preceding work\" and \"exercises in which muscular force opposes the inertia of the load and not its weight\", plus \"Thus, to develop speed-strength...\" summary. The pieces are in the loading entry and the arm-throw case, but neither states the two-lever summary. Optional one-sentence addition to `heuristic--speed-strength-loading-...` What it is.\n\n## Reciprocal-link suggestions\n\n- `heuristic--analytical-synthetic-and-multifaceted-preparation-suit-only-early-training` and `heuristic--choose-cross-training-by-fitness-qualities-and-phase-not-sport-label` (5.5).\n- `principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities` and `framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation` (5.4).\n- `framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric` and `heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size` (5.2.4).\n\n## Overall verdict\n\n**GO AFTER FIXES.** Required fixes:\n\n1. Newton II: flag ΔV as final velocity (equals change only from rest); \"net propulsive impulse\"; replace circular-motion example in (d) with constant-gravity projectile; \"net external force on the body\" in (c).\n2. Dynamic correspondence: fix \"paragraph before\" to \"paragraph that closes\" the dynamics discussion; label \"This passage is the authors' own statement ... Use it when\" as Inference.\n3. Three methods: say which load-rep pairing clashes (90-95%/3-6 clearly; 85-95%/3-5RM loosely); rewrite the line-105 fragment with an \"Eric's baseline:\" prefix.\n4. DeLorme: remove or label \"mostly in rehabilitation or untrained subjects\".\n5. Berger: correct the traceability sentence (conclusions 1 and 5 trace to Berger 1963 on p.259; only 6 is orphaned); add the 1-3 rep advanced-lifter sentence.\n6. Ivanov: retitle and rename (eccentric raised squat 1RM most; no \"own test\" for eccentric); label the \"trains the nervous system for that mode\" gloss.\n7. Implement mass: retitle and rename away from \"stay close to competition mass\"; fix When to apply; add the far-from-competition counterexamples (labelled Inference).\n8. After renames in 6 and 7, update inbound wikilinks in this batch.\n\nNo NO-GO. Both `contested` entries keep both sides on the cited pages. The single `proven` entry keeps its scope.\n(eval):1: ===== not found","is_error":true,"tool_use_id":"toolu_015JAwh8nNdvJgRwVD2bsRr6"}]},"uuid":"baa9f51e-2f83-4906-816c-600057db5039","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:12:40.819Z","toolUseResult":"Error: Exit code 1\n# Review: Supertraining Ch 5a (5.1 to 5.2.3), independent falsification pass\n\nReviewer: Claude Opus (independent of the writer). Date: 2026-10-03.\nScope: the 19 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch5a.txt`, folder `staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/`.\nSource: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx 266-278 (printed 255-267). Every page in range was rendered to `staging/_renders/r5a_p266.png` ... `r5a_p278.png` and opened as an image. Every equation, table, figure and number cited below was read from the image, not the text layer.\nBaseline: every \"Eric's baseline:\" quote checked against `docs/method-baseline.md` at the cited line (lines 61, 67, 73, 76, 78, 79, 96, 97, 101, 103, 104, 105, 107, 130, 134, 141, 181, 182, 188, 260). All quotes and line numbers match. Tension 16 is correctly named at line 260.\nLinks: every `[[wikilink]]` in the 19 files resolves to a file in the vault library or the staging library. Claims the entries make about linked entries were spot-checked (Bischke & Morehouse in the PAP entry; breath-holding in the isometric drawbacks entry; Moyer wearable-resistance entry \"wrong in three separate ways\"; Bondarchuk leg-to-leg 50 m \"all levels\"; Vasiliev uniform-gain in the threshold entry; APRE named in the isokinetic entry). All hold.\nDuplicates: searched the vaul