{"parentUuid":"bcb9e568-dce6-46fd-b06a-4d19b4a0887b","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"06ea19f5-0c14-4f73-9ed0-7b72125de0b9","agentId":"ad490fb3266cc50f1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TfUfaZDGuejo1GWrnubw3a","type":"tool_result","content":"claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve.md\nprinciple--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities.md\nheuristic--analytical-synthetic-and-multifaceted-preparation-suit-only-early-training.md\nheuristic--dynamic-correspondence-means-matching-motor-characteristics-not-copying-the-sport-movement.md\nframework--special-strength-methods-classified-by-sport-group-and-strength-type.md\nframework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric.md\nclaim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared.md\nclaim--berger-sets-reps-and-frequency-studies-in-novices.md\nclaim--moderate-and-mixed-movement-tempo-for-repetition-strength-gains.md\nclaim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves.md\nframework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise.md\nheuristic--apre-skill-learning-6rm-pyramid-test-and-rm-muscle-testing.md\nheuristic--isometric-apre-six-second-maximum-protocol.md\nframework--two-groups-of-speed-strength-movements-and-their-tension-types.md\nheuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix.md\nclaim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-stay-close-to-competition-mass.md\ncase-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work.md\nheuristic--cyclic-vs-acyclic-speed-strength-tempo-rest-and-fatigue.md\ncase-study--alternate-leg-bounding-with-thigh-cuffs-for-sprinters.md\n\n---\n# Fixes applied: Supertraining 4.3-4.4 (Ch4-3-4), 23 entries\n\nFixer: Claude Opus. Date: 2026-10-03. Review: `staging/review_supertraining_ch4-3-4_opus.md` (round 1, GO AFTER FIXES: 8 entry FIX rows plus 1 `_book.md` FIX).\nSource checked: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`. I extracted the text of PDF idx 254, 255, 256, 257, 258, 260, 263 and 264 with PyMuPDF. I rendered page images for PDF 255, 256, 257, 258, 260, 263 and 264 to `staging/_renders/fix_p<N>.png` and opened PDF 256 (Fig 4.22 and its passage), PDF 257 (power equations, Eq 4.1) and PDF 264 (Table 4.2). Page key: PDF idx = printed + 11.\nScope: the 23 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch4-3-4.txt`; only the 8 flagged entries and `_book.md` were edited. No new entries were written, so the scope file is unchanged.\n\n| file | what changed |\n|---|---|\n| claim--power-equals-force-times-velocity-only-when-force-is-constant.md | **Title** is now \"The Authors Warn That P = F x V Is Misapplied: Average or Peak Force Times Velocity Is Not Power When Force Varies; Use the Force-Time Curve, a Force Plate or High-Speed Video\". The slug is unchanged, so the one inbound link (RFD entry) still resolves.<br>**What it is:** the scope line now reads \"Scope of `proven`: only the identity P = dW/dt = F·v, which holds instant by instant for any force (Inference, see Why it works)\". It says the authors' constant-force statement is imprecise and is not part of that scope. Power is now quoted as \"The rate of doing work at any instant\" (PDF 257 image).<br>**Why it works:** rewritten in full. The old first sentence (\"This is calculus, and the authors state it ... wrong instantaneous picture\") endorsed the constant-force claim in the writer's voice. Now:<br>(a) The authors' two sentences are quoted with the locator (\"if F is constant over the interval concerned\"; \"If the force is not constant ... equation (4.1)\").<br>(b) The scope sentence follows.<br>(c) An Inference gives the physics: dW/dt = F·ds/dt = F·v at every instant. Constant force is needed only when one force value stands for a whole interval. What fails is multiplying an average or peak force by an average or peak velocity (the average of a product is not the product of averages, and the peaks need not coincide). The authors' \"It is inadequate to refer to average or peak force alone\" is quoted as the point the warning targets.<br>**When NOT:** \"The physics is sound; the wording around it is loose.\" is replaced by \"The authors' statement that P = F.V needs constant force is imprecise (see What it is); their practical warning against single average or peak values stands.\"<br>**Confidence:** `confidence: proven` kept, scoped to the instantaneous identity only (textbook mechanics, which the brief allows as `proven`). |\n| framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence.md | **When NOT:** the Inference \"when the criteria conflict (for example ... breaks the regime of work ...)\" is replaced. The new text says the book gives no ranking among the criteria, then quotes three sentences from PDF 256 / p.245: the \"incongruous and artificial (Fig 4.22)\" sentence, \"this occurs only when an exercise is attempted which fails to take into consideration the appropriate criteria\", and that the Fig 4.22 exercise \"may be appropriate if applied carefully\". Then: \"So they do not treat the criteria as conflicting. Inference: in practice, meeting all five at once may still force a compromise; the book does not address that case.\" |\n| heuristic--train-force-at-the-accentuated-joint-angle.md | **What it is:** the Fig 4.22 paragraph now quotes the full \"can sometimes lead to such initial positions using weights or pulley machines which are incongruous and artificial (Fig 4.22)\" sentence. It then adds the dropped \"However, this occurs only when an exercise is attempted which fails to take into consideration the appropriate criteria\" (PDF 256 / p.245). It also quotes the text's own account of the two partners (\"simulates the vertical loading which sprinting normally imposes\"; \"intended to strengthen the power of the hip flexors\"). The caption and \"applied carefully\" sentences are kept. |\n| heuristic--parachute-resistance-sizing-progression-and-contrast-release.md | **Why it works:** first sentence replaced. It now quotes the authors' \"increases rapidly with velocity (actually the square of the velocity)\" and \"allows us to use these devices more knowledgeably\", plus the next sentence on the page, \"Naturally, running speed determines the degree of drag resistance\" (PDF 255 / p.244), so the size-and-speed point is in their words. \"standard fluid mechanics\" is removed. The new Inference reads: \"velocity-squared drag is the textbook drag equation, and this relation is the only part of the entry that could rate `proven`\". It is merged with the existing Inference, so there is one, not two. |\n| claim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence.md | **When to apply:** the populations are now labelled: \"(Inference: for example, heavy athletes or athletes returning from lower-limb injury; the book names no population)\". \"(hurdlers, jumpers)\" is changed to \"(the book names hurdlers and jumpers)\". |\n| heuristic--same-exercise-different-regime-boxer-vs-shot-putter.md | **When to apply:** now reads \"single fast actions against a set resistance call for heavier loads and few repetitions (Inference: with full recovery between efforts; the book does not state rest).\" This matches the source's \"rapid single movements against a specific resistance\" (PDF 258 / p.247). |\n| case-study--high-jumper-factor-structure-by-qualification.md | **When to apply:** \"and less to plantarflexor power\" is removed. Added: \"Inference: the falling plantarflexor share may justify a smaller plantarflexor emphasis, but the book reports a factor share, not a training reduction.\" |\n| case-study--skater-factor-structure-by-qualification.md | **When NOT:** added \"Class 1 factor IV (2.7) is smaller than factor V (8.9), against the order of factors by share (verified against the page image, PDF p. 264 / printed p. 253)\", followed by a labelled Inference: the value may be a misprint, with the arithmetic (35.9 + 23.3 + 16.8 + 12.7 + 8.9 = 97.6), so treat 2.7 as unclear.<br>**When to apply:** the Inference no longer quotes \"2.7% to 36.5%\". It now says the rise to first factor at Master of Sport (36.5%) is consistent with the teaching point, and that the Class 1 value is unclear, \"so the size of the change should not be quoted.\" The table keeps the printed 2.7. |\n| _book.md | **Line 96:** now reads `| 4 | The Means of Special Strength Training | 201-254 | 212-265 | done | 64 (see section rows below) |`. 64 = 6 (4.1) + 35 (4.2) + 13 (4.3) + 10 (4.4). The 4.2.x sub-rows sum to 35. A count of `chapter: \"4` frontmatter on disk also gives 64 (6/35/13/10).<br>**Batch note:** deleted the sentence \"The Chapter 4 parent row still reads \"in progress \\| 6\" and was not changed in this session.\" No other line touched. |\n\n## Disagreements with the review\n\n1. **Power entry: the review's scope sentence is not used word for word.** The review says the constant-force form \"is true only when one force value stands for the whole interval\". That \"only\" is too strong: average power also equals average force times velocity when velocity is constant. I wrote \"Constant force is needed only when one force value stands for a whole interval: then power over the interval equals that F times the average velocity\", and put it inside the Inference because it is my physics, not the authors'. I also rewrote the first sentence of Why it works, which the review's fix (3) left in place. That sentence (\"This is calculus, and the authors state it: P = F.V is the special case ... with constant force\") still endorsed the imprecise claim in the writer's voice. The substance agrees with the review: the authors' constant-force condition is imprecise, and their warning about average or peak values stands.\n2. **Five-criteria entry: I did not adopt the review's paraphrase that \"awkward positions such as Fig 4.22 arise only when ...\".** The source reads two ways. It calls the Fig 4.22 positions \"incongruous and artificial\". It says that happens \"only when\" the criteria are ignored. It then says the Fig 4.22 exercise itself \"may be appropriate if applied carefully\" (PDF 256 / p.245, text and image). So the figure is both an example of awkward loading and a usable exercise. I quoted all three sentences rather than having the entry say Fig 4.22 is the ignored-criteria case. I agree with the review's core point: the authors deny a conflict among the criteria, and the old \"breaks the regime of work\" reading is not in the source.\n3. **Skater entry: misprint speculation labelled.** The review's text gives the \"2.7 may be a misprint (12.7 would restore the order ...)\" reasoning unlabelled. That is reviewer or fixer reasoning, so it is now an \"Inference:\" and carries the column sum.\n4. **Parachute sizing: one extra quote.** I added the \"Naturally, running speed determines the degree of drag resistance\" sentence (PDF 255 / p.244). Without it, \"so parachute size and running speed together set the load\" would sit as unlabelled writer voice.\n5. **OBLA (optional missing idea): not added as an entry.** Existing entries already cover it.\n   - `claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max` quotes \"Since OBLA depends on VO2 max, it is considered useful to strive to raise VO2 max ...\" (contested list) and the Christensen & Hogberg 1950 \"approximately 50% more effective\" sentence (When NOT).\n   - `claim--local-blood-flow-not-vo2max-tracks-competition-period-endurance-gains` quotes \"OBLA (lactate threshold) and VO2 max levels can change independently\" and \"OBLA can rise\" while VO2 max falls.\n   - The only uncaptured sentence is the definitional opener (PDF 260 / printed p. 249): \"Practical improvement of the metabolic processes in endurance sports is associated with increasing the potential to utilise aerobic sources of energy by raising the Onset of Blood Lactate Accumulation (OBLA), i.e. that level of oxygen consumption at which the anaerobic processes begin to be prominently activated.\"\n   - A stand-alone entry would mostly repeat those quotes. The sentence belongs in the What it is of the endurance entry at a later pass. That entry is GO, so it is outside this fix scope.\n\n...\n19:## Disagreements with the review\n20-\n21-1. **Power entry: the review's scope sentence is not used word for word.** The review says the constant-force form \"is true only when one force value stands for the whole interval\". That \"only\" is too strong: average power also equals average force times velocity when velocity is constant. I wrote \"Constant force is needed only when one force value stands for a whole interval: then power over the interval equals that F times the average velocity\", and put it inside the Inference because it is my physics, not the authors'. I also rewrote the first sentence of Why it works, which the review's fix (3) left in place. That sentence (\"This is calculus, and the authors state it: P = F.V is the special case ... with constant force\") still endorsed the imprecise claim in the writer's voice. The substance agrees with the review: the authors' constant-force condition is imprecise, and their warning about average or peak values stands.\n22-2. **Five-criteria entry: I did not adopt the review's paraphrase that \"awkward positions such as Fig 4.22 arise only when ...\".** The source reads two ways. It calls the Fig 4.22 positions \"incongruous and artificial\". It says that happens \"only when\" the criteria are ignored. It then says the Fig 4.22 exercise itself \"may be appropriate if applied carefully\" (PDF 256 / p.245, text and image). So the figure is both an example of awkward loading and a usable exercise. I quoted all three sentences rather than having the entry say Fig 4.22 is the ignored-criteria case. I agree with the review's core point: the authors deny a conflict among the criteria, and the old \"breaks the regime of work\" reading is not in the source.\n23-3. **Skater entry: misprint speculation labelled.** The review's text gives the \"2.7 may be a misprint (12.7 would restore the order ...)\" reasoning unlabelled. That is reviewer or fixer reasoning, so it is now an \"Inference:\" and carries the column sum.\n24-4. **Parachute sizing: one extra quote.** I added the \"Naturally, running speed determines the degree of drag resistance\" sentence (PDF 255 / p.244). Without it, \"so parachute size and running speed together set the load\" would sit as unlabelled writer voice.\n25-5. **OBLA (optional missing idea): not added as an entry.** Existing entries already cover it.\n26-   - `claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max` quotes \"Since OBLA depends on VO2 max, it is considered useful to strive to raise VO2 max ...\" (contested list) and the Christensen & Hogberg 1950 \"approximately 50% more effective\" sentence (When NOT).\n27-   - `claim--local-blood-flow-not-vo2max-tracks-competition-period-endurance-gains` quotes \"OBLA (lactate threshold) and VO2 max levels can change independently\" and \"OBLA can rise\" while VO2 max falls.\n(end)\n== review-history words:\n(end)\n== wrapped wikilinks:\n(end)\n== header count per file: all 23 files report 6\n```\n\nThere are no CR line endings in the book folder.\n\nLint: the original `scripts/lint_library.py` is not edited. It hard-codes `VAULT = Path(r\"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\SecondBrain\\Knowledge\\library\")` (line 12) and resolves `STAGING` relative to its own location. In a temp copy at `/tmp/flint_lint/lint_library_mac.py` I set VAULT to `/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library`. I also pinned STAGING to `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library`. Without that pin, the copy under `/tmp` resolved STAGING to `/staging/library`, and 68 links to staging-only entries were falsely reported as unresolved. Run from the Flint root:\n\n```\n$ PYTHONIOENCODING=utf-8 python3 /tmp/flint_lint/lint_library_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 114, 115]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1\n```\n\nThe one issue is the known `_book.md` false positive (title and table dashes), outside my edits.\n\nFor the promoter: confidence tallies are unchanged (the power entry stays `proven`, now scoped to the instantaneous identity). The power entry's slug still says \"only-when-force-is-constant\", which no longer matches its title. A rename at promotion would need the one Related link in `heuristic--rfd-as-correspondence-criterion-and-impulse-cannot-compare-movements.md` updated.\n\n## Coordinator change after the fix pass\n\nRenamed `claim--power-equals-force-times-velocity-only-when-force-is-constant.md` to `claim--average-or-peak-force-times-velocity-is-not-power.md` so the slug matches the corrected title. Updated the one link (in `heuristic--rfd-as-correspondence-criterion-and-impulse-cannot-compare-movements.md`) and the scope file. No other file linked it.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"917a2e94-0f73-467f-9e05-d1a7cb9c2102","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:12:49.028Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve.md\nprinciple--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities.md\nheuristic--analytical-synthetic-and-multifaceted-preparation-suit-only-early-training.md\nheuristic--dynamic-correspondence-means-matching-motor-characteristics-not-copying-the-sport-movement.md\nframework--special-strength-methods-classified-by-sport-group-and-strength-type.md\nframework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric.md\nclaim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared.md\nclaim--berger-sets-reps-and-frequency-studies-in-novices.md\nclaim--moderate-and-mixed-movement-tempo-for-repetition-strength-gains.md\nclaim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves.md\nframework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise.md\nheuristic--apre-skill-learning-6rm-pyramid-test-and-rm-muscle-testing.md\nheuristic--isometric-apre-six-second-maximum-protocol.md\nframework--two-groups-of-speed-strength-movements-and-their-tension-types.md\nheuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix.md\nclaim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-stay-close-to-competition-mass.md\ncase-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work.md\nheuristic--cyclic-vs-acyclic-speed-strength-tempo-rest-and-fatigue.md\ncase-study--alternate-leg-bounding-with-thigh-cuffs-for-sprinters.md\n\n---\n# Fixes applied: Supertraining 4.3-4.4 (Ch4-3-4), 23 entries\n\nFixer: Claude Opus. Date: 2026-10-03. Review: `staging/review_supertraining_ch4-3-4_opus.md` (round 1, GO AFTER FIXES: 8 entry FIX rows plus 1 `_book.md` FIX).\nSource checked: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`. I extracted the text of PDF idx 254, 255, 256, 257, 258, 260, 263 and 264 with PyMuPDF. I rendered page images for PDF 255, 256, 257, 258, 260, 263 and 264 to `staging/_renders/fix_p<N>.png` and opened PDF 256 (Fig 4.22 and its passage), PDF 257 (power equations, Eq 4.1) and PDF 264 (Table 4.2). Page key: PDF idx = printed + 11.\nScope: the 23 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch4-3-4.txt`; only the 8 flagged entries and `_book.md` were edited. No new entries were written, so the scope file is unchanged.\n\n| file | what changed |\n|---|---|\n| claim--power-equals-force-times-velocity-only-when-force-is-constant.md | **Title** is now \"The Authors Warn That P = F x V Is Misapplied: Average or Peak Force Times Velocity Is Not Power When Force Varies; Use the Force-Time Curve, a Force Plate or High-Speed Video\". The slug is unchanged, so the one inbound link (RFD entry) still resolves.<br>**What it is:** the scope line now reads \"Scope of `proven`: only the identity P = dW/dt = F·v, which holds instant by instant for any force (Inference, see Why it works)\". It says the authors' constant-force statement is imprecise and is not part of that scope. Power is now quoted as \"The rate of doing work at any instant\" (PDF 257 image).<br>**Why it works:** rewritten in full. The old first sentence (\"This is calculus, and the authors state it ... wrong instantaneous picture\") endorsed the constant-force claim in the writer's voice. Now:<br>(a) The authors' two sentences are quoted with the locator (\"if F is constant over the interval concerned\"; \"If the force is not constant ... equation (4.1)\").<br>(b) The scope sentence follows.<br>(c) An Inference gives the physics: dW/dt = F·ds/dt = F·v at every instant. Constant force is needed only when one force value stands for a whole interval. What fails is multiplying an average or peak force by an average or peak velocity (the average of a product is not the product of averages, and the peaks need not coincide). The authors' \"It is inadequate to refer to average or peak force alone\" is quoted as the point the warning targets.<br>**When NOT:** \"The physics is sound; the wording around it is loose.\" is replaced by \"The authors' statement that P = F.V needs constant force is imprecise (see What it is); their practical warning against single average or peak values stands.\"<br>**Confidence:** `confidence: proven` kept, scoped to the instantaneous identity only (textbook mechanics, which the brief allows as `proven`). |\n| framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence.md | **When NOT:** the Inference \"when the criteria conflict (for example ... breaks the regime of work ...)\" is replaced. The new text says the book gives no ranking among the criteria, then quotes three sentences from PDF 256 / p.245: the \"incongruous and artificial (Fig 4.22)\" sentence, \"this occurs only when an exercise is attempted which fails to take into consideration the appropriate criteria\", and that the Fig 4.22 exercise \"may be appropriate if applied carefully\". Then: \"So they do not treat the criteria as conflicting. Inference: in practice, meeting all five at once may still force a compromise; the book does not address that case.\" |\n| heuristic--train-force-at-the-accentuated-joint-angle.md | **What it is:** the Fig 4.22 paragraph now quotes the full \"can sometimes lead to such initial positions using weights or pulley machines which are incongruous and artificial (Fig 4.22)\" sentence. It then adds the dropped \"However, this occurs only when an exercise is attempted which fails to take into consideration the appropriate criteria\" (PDF 256 / p.245). It also quotes the text's own account of the two partners (\"simulates the vertical loading which sprinting normally imposes\"; \"intended to strengthen the power of the hip flexors\"). The caption and \"applied carefully\" sentences are kept. |\n| heuristic--parachute-resistance-sizing-progression-and-contrast-release.md | **Why it works:** first sentence replaced. It now quotes the authors' \"increases rapidly with velocity (actually the square of the velocity)\" and \"allows us to use these devices more knowledgeably\", plus the next sentence on the page, \"Naturally, running speed determines the degree of drag resistance\" (PDF 255 / p.244), so the size-and-speed point is in their words. \"standard fluid mechanics\" is removed. The new Inference reads: \"velocity-squared drag is the textbook drag equation, and this relation is the only part of the entry that could rate `proven`\". It is merged with the existing Inference, so there is one, not two. |\n| claim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence.md | **When to apply:** the populations are now labelled: \"(Inference: for example, heavy athletes or athletes returning from lower-limb injury; the book names no population)\". \"(hurdlers, jumpers)\" is changed to \"(the book names hurdlers and jumpers)\". |\n| heuristic--same-exercise-different-regime-boxer-vs-shot-putter.md | **When to apply:** now reads \"single fast actions against a set resistance call for heavier loads and few repetitions (Inference: with full recovery between efforts; the book does not state rest).\" This matches the source's \"rapid single movements against a specific resistance\" (PDF 258 / p.247). |\n| case-study--high-jumper-factor-structure-by-qualification.md | **When to apply:** \"and less to plantarflexor power\" is removed. Added: \"Inference: the falling plantarflexor share may justify a smaller plantarflexor emphasis, but the book reports a factor share, not a training reduction.\" |\n| case-study--skater-factor-structure-by-qualification.md | **When NOT:** added \"Class 1 factor IV (2.7) is smaller than factor V (8.9), against the order of factors by share (verified against the page image, PDF p. 264 / printed p. 253)\", followed by a labelled Inference: the value may be a misprint, with the arithmetic (35.9 + 23.3 + 16.8 + 12.7 + 8.9 = 97.6), so treat 2.7 as unclear.<br>**When to apply:** the Inference no longer quotes \"2.7% to 36.5%\". It now says the rise to first factor at Master of Sport (36.5%) is consistent with the teaching point, and that the Class 1 value is unclear, \"so the size of the change should not be quoted.\" The table keeps the printed 2.7. |\n| _book.md | **Line 96:** now reads `| 4 | The Means of Special Strength Training | 201-254 | 212-265 | done | 64 (see section rows below) |`. 64 = 6 (4.1) + 35 (4.2) + 13 (4.3) + 10 (4.4). The 4.2.x sub-rows sum to 35. A count of `chapter: \"4` frontmatter on disk also gives 64 (6/35/13/10).<br>**Batch note:** deleted the sentence \"The Chapter 4 parent row still reads \"in progress \\| 6\" and was not changed in this session.\" No other line touched. |\n\n## Disagreements with the review\n\n1. **Power entry: the review's scope sentence is not used word for word.** The review says the constant-force form \"is true only when one force value stands for the whole interval\". That \"only\" is too strong: average power also equals average force times velocity when velocity is constant. I wrote \"Constant force is needed only when one force value stands for a whole interval: then power over the interval equals that F times the average velocity\", and put it inside the Inference because it is my physics, not the authors'. I also rewrote the first sentence of Why it works, which the review's fix (3) left in place. That sentence (\"This is calculus, and the authors state it: P = F.V is the special case ... with constant force\") still endorsed the imprecise claim in the writer's voice. The substance agrees with the review: the authors' constant-force condition is imprecise, and their warning about average or peak values stands.\n2. **Five-criteria entry: I did not adopt the review's paraphrase that \"awkward positions such as Fig 4.22 arise only when ...\".** The source reads two ways. It calls the Fig 4.22 positions \"incongruous and artificial\". It says that happens \"only when\" the criteria are ignored. It then says the Fig 4.22 exercise itself \"may be appropriate if applied carefully\" (PDF 256 / p.245, text and image). So the figure is both an example of awkward loading and a usable exercise. I quoted all three sentences rather than having the entry say Fig 4.22 is the ignored-criteria case. I agree with the review's core point: the authors deny a conflict among the criteria, and the old \"breaks the regime of work\" reading is not in the source.\n3. **Skater entry: misprint speculation labelled.** The review's text gives the \"2.7 may be a misprint (12.7 would restore the order ...)\" reasoning unlabelled. That is reviewer or fixer reasoning, so it is now an \"Inference:\" and carries the column sum.\n4. **Parachute sizing: one extra quote.** I added the \"Naturally, running speed determines the degree of drag resistance\" sentence (PDF 255 / p.244). Without it, \"so parachute size and running speed together set the load\" would sit as unlabelled writer voice.\n5. **OBLA (optional missing idea): not added as an entry.** Existing entries already cover it.\n   - `claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max` quotes \"Since OBLA depends on VO2 max, it is considered useful to strive to raise VO2 max ...\" (contested list) and the Christensen & Hogberg 1950 \"approximately 50% more effective\" sentence (When NOT).\n   - `claim--local-blood-flow-not-vo2max-tracks-competition-period-endurance-gains` quotes \"OBLA (lactate threshold) and VO2 max levels can change independently\" and \"OBLA can rise\" while VO2 max falls.\n   - The only uncaptured sentence is the definitional opener (PDF 260 / printed p. 249): \"Practical improvement of the metabolic processes in endurance sports is associated with increasing the potential to utilise aerobic sources of energy by raising the Onset of Blood Lactate Accumulation (OBLA), i.e. that level of oxygen consumption at which the anaerobic processes begin to be prominently activated.\"\n   - A stand-alone entry would mostly repeat those quotes. The sentence belongs in the What it is of the endurance entry at a later pass. That entry is GO, so it is outside this fix scope.\n\n...\n19:## Disagreements with the review\n20-\n21-1. **Power entry: the review's scope sentence is not used word for word.** The review says the constant-force form \"is true only when one force value stands for the whole interval\". That \"only\" is too strong: average power also equals average force times velocity when velocity is constant. I wrote \"Constant force is needed only when one force value stands for a whole interval: then power over the interval equals that F times the average velocity\", and put it inside the Inference because it is my physics, not the authors'. I also rewrote the first sentence of Why it works, which the review's fix (3) left in place. That sentence (\"This is calculus, and the authors state it: P = F.V is the special case ... with constant force\") still endorsed the imprecise claim in the writer's voice. The substance agrees with the review: the authors' constant-force condition is imprecise, and their warning about average or peak values stands.\n22-2. **Five-criteria entry: I did not adopt the review's paraphrase that \"awkward positions such as Fig 4.22 arise only when ...\".** The source reads two ways. It calls the Fig 4.22 positions \"incongruous and artificial\". It says that happens \"only when\" the criteria are ignored. It then says the Fig 4.22 exercise itself \"may be appropriate if applied carefully\" (PDF 256 / p.245, text and image). So the figure is both an example of awkward loading and a usable exercise. I quoted all three sentences rather than having the entry say Fig 4.22 is the ignored-criteria case. I agree with the review's core point: the authors deny a conflict among the criteria, and the old \"breaks the regime of work\" reading is not in the source.\n23-3. **Skater entry: misprint speculation labelled.** The review's text gives the \"2.7 may be a misprint (12.7 would restore the order ...)\" reasoning unlabelled. That is reviewer or fixer reasoning, so it is now an \"Inference:\" and carries the column sum.\n24-4. **Parachute sizing: one extra quote.** I added the \"Naturally, running speed determines the degree of drag resistance\" sentence (PDF 255 / p.244). Without it, \"so parachute size and running speed together set the load\" would sit as unlabelled writer voice.\n25-5. **OBLA (optional missing idea): not added as an entry.** Existing entries already cover it.\n26-   - `claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max` quotes \"Since OBLA depends on VO2 max, it is considered useful to strive to raise VO2 max ...\" (contested list) and the Christensen & Hogberg 1950 \"approximately 50% more effective\" sentence (When NOT).\n27-   - `claim--local-blood-flow-not-vo2max-tracks-competition-period-endurance-gains` quotes \"OBLA (lactate threshold) and VO2 max levels can change independently\" and \"OBLA can rise\" while VO2 max falls.\n(end)\n== review-history words:\n(end)\n== wrapped wikilinks:\n(end)\n== header count per file: all 23 files report 6\n```\n\nThere are no CR line endings in the book folder.\n\nLint: the original `scripts/lint_library.py` is not edited. It hard-codes `VAULT = Path(r\"C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\SecondBrain\\Knowledge\\library\")` (line 12) and resolves `STAGING` relative to its own location. In a temp copy at `/tmp/flint_lint/lint_library_mac.py` I set VAULT to `/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library`. I also pinned STAGING to `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library`. Without that pin, the copy under `/tmp` resolved STAGING to `/staging/library`, and 68 links to staging-only entries were falsely reported as unresolved. Run from the Flint root:\n\n```\n$ PYTHONIOENCODING=utf-8 python3 /tmp/flint_lint/lint_library_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 114, 115]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1\n```\n\nThe one issue is the known `_book.md` false positive (title and table dashes), outside my edits.\n\nFor the promoter: confidence tallies are unchanged (the power entry stays `proven`, now scoped to the instantaneous identity). The power entry's slug still says \"only-when-force-is-constant\", which no longer matches its title. A rename at promotion would need the one Related link in `heuristic--rfd-as-correspondence-criterion-and-impulse-cannot-compare-movements.md` updated.\n\n## Coordinator change after the fix pass\n\nRenamed `claim--power-equals-force-times-velocity-only-when-force-is-constant.md` to `claim--average-or-peak-force-times-velocity-is-not-power.md` so the slug matches the corrected title. Updated the one link (in `heuristic--rfd-as-correspondence-criterion-and-impulse-cannot-compare-movements.md`) and the scope file. No other file linked it.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":