{"parentUuid":"670786ed-9e48-46eb-b3f2-bd5626c463db","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ad490fb3266cc50f1","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffbhZMX1LmP7kBzqXqRL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LDRnLrzC9vKgQcnJNaEsTS","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; for f in heuristic--dynamic-correspondence-means-matching-motor-characteristics-not-copying-the-sport-movement.md framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric.md; do echo \"=== $f\"; sed -n '14,40p' $f; done","description":"Read dynamic correspondence and three methods entries"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2953,"cache_read_input_tokens":81199,"output_tokens":1470,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1181},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2953},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1470,"cache_read_input_tokens":81199,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2953,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2953,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01LDRnLrzC9vKgQcnJNaEsTS":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; for f in heuristic--dynamic-correspondence-means-matching-motor-characteristics-not-copying-the-sport-movement.md framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric.md; do echo \"=== $f\"; sed -n '14,40p' $f; done","description":"Read dynamic correspondence and three methods entries"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffbhYPxxMW1A1yMeUYAY","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"469074a8-7f07-4d7e-b4c6-991f752a6dc4","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:13:15.830Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"469074a8-7f07-4d7e-b4c6-991f752a6dc4","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"06ea19f5-0c14-4f73-9ed0-7b72125de0b9","agentId":"ad490fb3266cc50f1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LDRnLrzC9vKgQcnJNaEsTS","type":"tool_result","content":"=== heuristic--dynamic-correspondence-means-matching-motor-characteristics-not-copying-the-sport-movement.md\n# Dynamic Correspondence Means Matching the Sport's Motor Characteristics, Not Copying the Sport Movement Under Load\n\n**What it is** — The authors warn of \"an excessive passion for making the strength training means correspond structurally to the special exercises.\" The misinterpretation of dynamic correspondence \"lies in literally copying the special exercise in training movements, instead of selecting a specific arsenal of means corresponding to the most important motor characteristics of the given sport and controlling their growth by creating conditions under which they will develop steadily.\" In their words, \"it is inappropriate to try to simulate sports specific movements with supplementary training activities,\" because this \"not only does ... not accurately develop the necessary fitness and motor abilities, but it can also alter the neuromuscular programmes which control the motor actions.\" The aim is \"the specific type of fitness and motor characteristics of the sport\" (PDF p. 268 / printed p. 257, verified against the page image). The paragraph before 5.1's dynamics discussion sets this up: the shape of the force-time curve depends on the neuromuscular system's ability to develop force \"with the speed necessary to produce the required motor effect,\" which \"requires specialised means of training,\" and the authors go as far as \"All subsequent training and performance errors are caused by inappropriate neuromuscular programming\" (PDF p. 267-268 / printed p. 256-257).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is that a loaded copy of a skill changes the skill's neuromuscular programme while missing the motor characteristic that matters. They give no further mechanism or data on these pages. Inference: the five criteria in 4.3 (amplitude and direction, accentuated region, dynamics of effort, rate and time of force, regime of work) are the tool for picking a \"specific arsenal of means\" by characteristic rather than by look.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: the Principle of Dynamic Correspondence \"governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 103, Explicit, Q1). This passage is the authors' own statement of that \"not surface resemblance\" rule. Use it when a coach asks for a loaded version of a sport skill (a weighted throwing motion, a banded route stem, a loaded sprint drill that changes the mechanics) and the question is whether it trains the quality or only resembles the skill.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors do not ban all resisted sport movements: in 4.3 they discuss parachute resistance for sprinters (see [[claim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence]]), and in 5.2.3 they write that \"good results are achieved by making the special exercise more demanding,\" with water resistance in rowing or very small limb loads enhancing speed-strength \"under conditions that are very close to those of the special exercise\" (PDF p. 277-278 / printed p. 266-267). Inference: the line is whether the load changes the movement's structure; light loads that leave the pattern intact fall outside this warning. The sweeping sentence \"All subsequent training and performance errors are caused by inappropriate neuromuscular programming\" is an assertion without evidence on the page; do not quote it as a finding.\n\n**Example** — Inference: the source gives no named example here. A heavy cable \"simulated\" football block that slows and reshapes the punch would be the literal copy the authors warn against; a heavy push or pull that matches the punch's accentuated region and rate of force, plus fast unloaded punches, would be the \"arsenal of means\" approach.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]]; [[heuristic--train-coupled-hip-flexion-extension-without-heavily-loaded-simulation]]; [[claim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence]]; [[principle--specificity-of-training-ten-dimensions]]; [[elite-sports-performance--evans/case-study--dynamic-correspondence-applied-to-wrestling-takedown]]\n=== framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric.md\n# Three Methods for Maximum Strength (Repetitive Effort, Brief Maximal Tension, Isometric) and When Each Is Used\n\n**What it is** — Maximum strength in this chapter is \"the maximal force that a person can develop voluntarily,\" shown \"under isometric conditions or in a very slow movement against a maximal load.\" There are \"two basic methods\": \"the method of repetitive effort and the method of brief maximal tension\" (PDF p. 269 / printed p. 258), with isometrics discussed as a third option (PDF p. 271-272 / printed p. 260-261). The authors' summary (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261, verified against the page image):\n- **Repetitive effort**: lifting a weight that is raised as strength grows; \"appropriate in the initial stages of strength development and when strength and its speed of production are not important.\" Moderate weights \"(50-60% of 1RM)\" for \"8-12\" reps increase muscle mass; heavy weights \"(90-95% of 1RM)\" for \"3-6\" reps raise strength faster with a smaller mass gain. The effect grows with \"the magnitude of the load and the volume of work.\" The authors attribute its effect to proprioceptive feedback from slow lifting, strong cortex-to-muscle excitation and more motor units recruited (Kabat 1947; Hellebrant & Houtz 1956; PDF p. 269 / printed p. 258).\n- **Brief maximal tension**: \"heavy weights (85-95% of 1RM, i.e. 3-5RM)\" combined with lighter weights in the session and maximal singles \"once or twice a week\"; weightlifters \"execute 5-6 exercises for 6-10 sets of 1-3 repetitions in one training session (Medvedev, 1967; Vorobyev, 1971)\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260). It raises maximum strength \"without appreciably increasing muscle mass,\" improves \"the ability to display strength quickly,\" suits athletes needing relative strength, and is used \"when the repetitive effort method has become ineffective,\" when strength must rise \"quickly in a relatively short period with a small volume of work,\" and to maintain strength and \"sporting form\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261).\n- **Isometric tension (with gradual increase in effort)**: maximum strength without mass gain; to maintain strength \"where speed of movement is not important,\" in preparatory training, and \"during the early stages of musculoskeletal rehabilitation, when movement might be restricted.\" Isometric tension \"may be applied slowly or explosively (see 4.2.4)\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' explanation is neuromuscular specificity: \"Similar significant increases in maximum strength can be obtained\" by all three, \"However, the strength acquired from each of these methods has its own neuromuscular specificity\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). For brief maximal tension they say it \"develops the ability to concentrate neuromuscular effort\" and that lifting near-maximal weights \"enhances the ability to accelerate heavy loads\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260). They also note that strength \"is retained for longer if it is founded first upon a base of increased muscle hypertrophy\" and declines faster if built mainly on neuromuscular efficiency (PDF p. 269 / printed p. 258); that retention point is atomized in [[claim--strength-detraining-and-retention-timelines]].\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: \"No sport performs true 1RM testing. Maxes are projected from observation plus the athlete's history\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 61, Explicit, Q10), and %1RM prescription is kept \"stated explicitly as built on projected maxes\" (line 260, Tension 16). Inference: the percentage bands here are therefore percentages of a projected max, so the RM descriptions (3-5RM, 8-12 reps) are the safer way to apply them. The baseline's off-season rule (\"relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work,\" line 105, course material, Inferred). Inference: that rule lines up with the authors' sequence, repetitive effort first and brief maximal tension once it stops working. Inference: the in-season rule \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" (line 105) matches the authors' use of brief maximal tension \"for maintaining the existing level of strength.\"\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The book contradicts itself on several details; keep both sides. (1) Sets: brief maximal tension needs sets \"increased to more than three (Berger, 1962)\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260), but its effect \"is enhanced by increasing the maximal weight, increasing the average weight lifted per session and reducing the number of sets and repetitions\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). (2) Size of effect: brief maximal tension \"yields a larger training effect than the progressive resistance method for developing maximum strength\" (Diachkov 1961; Chudinov 1961; PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260), against \"Similar significant increases in maximum strength can be obtained by the repetitive effort and brief maximal tension methods, as well as with isometric tension\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). (3) Isometrics: isometric training \"does not increase strength significantly more\" than dynamic lifting (Salter 1955; PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260) and dynamic 3 x 5-6 \"is more effective than the corresponding amount of isometric work\" (Rasch & Morehouse 1957; Berger 1962; PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261), against the \"similar significant increases ... with isometric tension\" sentence on the same page. (4) Load-rep pairings: the 5RM is \"about 89%\" of 1RM (PDF p. 269 / printed p. 258), yet \"90-95% of 1RM\" is paired with \"3-6\" reps (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261), and \"85-95% of 1RM\" with \"3-5RM\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260). These three are not consistent; do not use any one as a rep-to-load conversion. Inference: for sports decided by fast force production, these three methods are not the whole answer; the authors put speed-strength and explosive work in separate method groups (5.2.3-5.2.4).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own prescription for weightlifters under brief maximal tension: \"5-6 exercises for 6-10 sets of 1-3 repetitions in one training session (Medvedev, 1967; Vorobyev, 1971)\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared]]; [[claim--berger-sets-reps-and-frequency-studies-in-novices]]; [[claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves]]; [[claim--strength-detraining-and-retention-timelines]]; [[framework--types-of-maximal-strength]]; [[framework--sarcomere-vs-sarcoplasmic-hypertrophy]]; [[heuristic--isometric-training-execution-protocol-and-advantages-drawbacks]]","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"12ae10a6-7357-46d1-9fa4-73285a779935","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:13:16.049Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"=== heuristic--dynamic-correspondence-means-matching-motor-characteristics-not-copying-the-sport-movement.md\n# Dynamic Correspondence Means Matching the Sport's Motor Characteristics, Not Copying the Sport Movement Under Load\n\n**What it is** — The authors warn of \"an excessive passion for making the strength training means correspond structurally to the special exercises.\" The misinterpretation of dynamic correspondence \"lies in literally copying the special exercise in training movements, instead of selecting a specific arsenal of means corresponding to the most important motor characteristics of the given sport and controlling their growth by creating conditions under which they will develop steadily.\" In their words, \"it is inappropriate to try to simulate sports specific movements with supplementary training activities,\" because this \"not only does ... not accurately develop the necessary fitness and motor abilities, but it can also alter the neuromuscular programmes which control the motor actions.\" The aim is \"the specific type of fitness and motor characteristics of the sport\" (PDF p. 268 / printed p. 257, verified against the page image). The paragraph before 5.1's dynamics discussion sets this up: the shape of the force-time curve depends on the neuromuscular system's ability to develop force \"with the speed necessary to produce the required motor effect,\" which \"requires specialised means of training,\" and the authors go as far as \"All subsequent training and performance errors are caused by inappropriate neuromuscular programming\" (PDF p. 267-268 / printed p. 256-257).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is that a loaded copy of a skill changes the skill's neuromuscular programme while missing the motor characteristic that matters. They give no further mechanism or data on these pages. Inference: the five criteria in 4.3 (amplitude and direction, accentuated region, dynamics of effort, rate and time of force, regime of work) are the tool for picking a \"specific arsenal of means\" by characteristic rather than by look.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: the Principle of Dynamic Correspondence \"governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 103, Explicit, Q1). This passage is the authors' own statement of that \"not surface resemblance\" rule. Use it when a coach asks for a loaded version of a sport skill (a weighted throwing motion, a banded route stem, a loaded sprint drill that changes the mechanics) and the question is whether it trains the quality or only resembles the skill.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors do not ban all resisted sport movements: in 4.3 they discuss parachute resistance for sprinters (see [[claim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence]]), and in 5.2.3 they write that \"good results are achieved by making the special exercise more demanding,\" with water resistance in rowing or very small limb loads enhancing speed-strength \"under conditions that are very close to those of the special exercise\" (PDF p. 277-278 / printed p. 266-267). Inference: the line is whether the load changes the movement's structure; light loads that leave the pattern intact fall outside this warning. The sweeping sentence \"All subsequent training and performance errors are caused by inappropriate neuromuscular programming\" is an assertion without evidence on the page; do not quote it as a finding.\n\n**Example** — Inference: the source gives no named example here. A heavy cable \"simulated\" football block that slows and reshapes the punch would be the literal copy the authors warn against; a heavy push or pull that matches the punch's accentuated region and rate of force, plus fast unloaded punches, would be the \"arsenal of means\" approach.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]]; [[heuristic--train-coupled-hip-flexion-extension-without-heavily-loaded-simulation]]; [[claim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence]]; [[principle--specificity-of-training-ten-dimensions]]; [[elite-sports-performance--evans/case-study--dynamic-correspondence-applied-to-wrestling-takedown]]\n=== framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric.md\n# Three Methods for Maximum Strength (Repetitive Effort, Brief Maximal Tension, Isometric) and When Each Is Used\n\n**What it is** — Maximum strength in this chapter is \"the maximal force that a person can develop voluntarily,\" shown \"under isometric conditions or in a very slow movement against a maximal load.\" There are \"two basic methods\": \"the method of repetitive effort and the method of brief maximal tension\" (PDF p. 269 / printed p. 258), with isometrics discussed as a third option (PDF p. 271-272 / printed p. 260-261). The authors' summary (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261, verified against the page image):\n- **Repetitive effort**: lifting a weight that is raised as strength grows; \"appropriate in the initial stages of strength development and when strength and its speed of production are not important.\" Moderate weights \"(50-60% of 1RM)\" for \"8-12\" reps increase muscle mass; heavy weights \"(90-95% of 1RM)\" for \"3-6\" reps raise strength faster with a smaller mass gain. The effect grows with \"the magnitude of the load and the volume of work.\" The authors attribute its effect to proprioceptive feedback from slow lifting, strong cortex-to-muscle excitation and more motor units recruited (Kabat 1947; Hellebrant & Houtz 1956; PDF p. 269 / printed p. 258).\n- **Brief maximal tension**: \"heavy weights (85-95% of 1RM, i.e. 3-5RM)\" combined with lighter weights in the session and maximal singles \"once or twice a week\"; weightlifters \"execute 5-6 exercises for 6-10 sets of 1-3 repetitions in one training session (Medvedev, 1967; Vorobyev, 1971)\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260). It raises maximum strength \"without appreciably increasing muscle mass,\" improves \"the ability to display strength quickly,\" suits athletes needing relative strength, and is used \"when the repetitive effort method has become ineffective,\" when strength must rise \"quickly in a relatively short period with a small volume of work,\" and to maintain strength and \"sporting form\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261).\n- **Isometric tension (with gradual increase in effort)**: maximum strength without mass gain; to maintain strength \"where speed of movement is not important,\" in preparatory training, and \"during the early stages of musculoskeletal rehabilitation, when movement might be restricted.\" Isometric tension \"may be applied slowly or explosively (see 4.2.4)\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' explanation is neuromuscular specificity: \"Similar significant increases in maximum strength can be obtained\" by all three, \"However, the strength acquired from each of these methods has its own neuromuscular specificity\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). For brief maximal tension they say it \"develops the ability to concentrate neuromuscular effort\" and that lifting near-maximal weights \"enhances the ability to accelerate heavy loads\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260). They also note that strength \"is retained for longer if it is founded first upon a base of increased muscle hypertrophy\" and declines faster if built mainly on neuromuscular efficiency (PDF p. 269 / printed p. 258); that retention point is atomized in [[claim--strength-detraining-and-retention-timelines]].\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: \"No sport performs true 1RM testing. Maxes are projected from observation plus the athlete's history\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 61, Explicit, Q10), and %1RM prescription is kept \"stated explicitly as built on projected maxes\" (line 260, Tension 16). Inference: the percentage bands here are therefore percentages of a projected max, so the RM descriptions (3-5RM, 8-12 reps) are the safer way to apply them. The baseline's off-season rule (\"relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work,\" line 105, course material, Inferred). Inference: that rule lines up with the authors' sequence, repetitive effort first and brief maximal tension once it stops working. Inference: the in-season rule \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" (line 105) matches the authors' use of brief maximal tension \"for maintaining the existing level of strength.\"\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The book contradicts itself