{"parentUuid":"0d5b4106-7b96-4900-b202-0b20419190c5","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ad490fb3266cc50f1","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffbmFb3eNdKw4uw1k5Hp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Ke94K9kqcP1zLv6PFyddYH","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; sed -n '14,18p' heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix.md","description":"Read speed-strength loading entry What it is"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6124,"cache_read_input_tokens":114621,"output_tokens":779,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":523},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6124},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":779,"cache_read_input_tokens":114621,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6124,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6124,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01Ke94K9kqcP1zLv6PFyddYH":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; sed -n '14,18p' heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix.md","description":"Read speed-strength loading entry What it is"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011Cffbm8ZsH5i4yFyJoEkYd","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"5d2d8875-4926-403e-8158-6a449d006269","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:14:00.060Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"5d2d8875-4926-403e-8158-6a449d006269","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"06ea19f5-0c14-4f73-9ed0-7b72125de0b9","agentId":"ad490fb3266cc50f1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ke94K9kqcP1zLv6PFyddYH","type":"tool_result","content":"# Speed-Strength Loading: Mostly About 20% of 1RM at Maximal Acceleration, Mixed 5:1 With Loads up to 40%, Plus Fast Isometrics at 60-80%\n\n**What it is** — \"The development of speed-strength is more effective the more speed loading there is in training and the shorter the time spent using slow movements (Yakovlev et al, 1960).\" \"Exercise with small loads (with approximately 20% of 1RM) is the principal method of developing speed-strength\" (Korobkov 1953; Vasiliev 1954; Gerasimov, Yakhontov 1954; Zimkin 1956; Agdgomelashvili 1964; Butenko 1967) (PDF p. 275-276 / printed p. 264-265, verified against the page images). With it, \"speed of movement increases with and without loads, and experiments have shown that the general increase in speed can reach 146% of the initial level.\" \"Movements should be executed with maximal effort and the apparatus should be accelerated as rapidly as possible.\" To promote recruitment, light and heavier weights \"(up to 40% of 1RM) should be combined (the variable method)\" with \"emphasis on acceleration at the beginning of the movement,\" plus plyometrics and \"exercises where isometric tension is generated quickly using 60-80% of 1RM.\" \"The optimal combination of the volume of exercises with light weights and relatively heavy weights should be in a ratio of 5:1,\" and \"the alternation of exercises is most effective for the ranked athlete\" (PDF p. 276 / printed p. 265). The section summary repeats it: \"exercises, primarily with small resistance (20% of 1RM) and, for acyclic activities of brief duration, a combination of these exercises with weights up to 40% of 1RM in a ratio of 5 to 1,\" with the regime matching the special exercise (cyclic or acyclic) and its starting condition (\"muscles relaxed, in a state of preliminary tension or stretched\") (PDF p. 278 / printed p. 267). The load-versus-speed conflict may be resolved if strength opposes \"not the weight (gravitational force), but its inertia,\" a method \"very seldom used in practice\" (PDF p. 277 / printed p. 266).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons are training specificity (more fast loading, less slow work) and recruitment \"in the active state\" from mixing light and heavier loads. They give no physiological mechanism beyond that.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"b57ea527-f05a-4269-8aa9-67fd1ffe4c21","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:14:00.247Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# Speed-Strength Loading: Mostly About 20% of 1RM at Maximal Acceleration, Mixed 5:1 With Loads up to 40%, Plus Fast Isometrics at 60-80%\n\n**What it is** — \"The development of speed-strength is more effective the more speed loading there is in training and the shorter the time spent using slow movements (Yakovlev et al, 1960).\" \"Exercise with small loads (with approximately 20% of 1RM) is the principal method of developing speed-strength\" (Korobkov 1953; Vasiliev 1954; Gerasimov, Yakhontov 1954; Zimkin 1956; Agdgomelashvili 1964; Butenko 1967) (PDF p. 275-276 / printed p. 264-265, verified against the page images). With it, \"speed of movement increases with and without loads, and experiments have shown that the general increase in speed can reach 146% of the initial level.\" \"Movements should be executed with maximal effort and the apparatus should be accelerated as rapidly as possible.\" To promote recruitment, light and heavier weights \"(up to 40% of 1RM) should be combined (the variable method)\" with \"emphasis on acceleration at the beginning of the movement,\" plus plyometrics and \"exercises where isometric tension is generated quickly using 60-80% of 1RM.\" \"The optimal combination of the volume of exercises with light weights and relatively heavy weights should be in a ratio of 5:1,\" and \"the alternation of exercises is most effective for the ranked athlete\" (PDF p. 276 / printed p. 265). The section summary repeats it: \"exercises, primarily with small resistance (20% of 1RM) and, for acyclic activities of brief duration, a combination of these exercises with weights up to 40% of 1RM in a ratio of 5 to 1,\" with the regime matching the special exercise (cyclic or acyclic) and its starting condition (\"muscles relaxed, in a state of preliminary tension or stretched\") (PDF p. 278 / printed p. 267). The load-versus-speed conflict may be resolved if strength opposes \"not the weight (gravitational force), but its inertia,\" a method \"very seldom used in practice\" (PDF p. 277 / printed p. 266).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons are training specificity (more fast loading, less slow work) and recruitment \"in the active state\" from mixing light and heavier loads. They give no physiological mechanism beyond that.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"5d2d8875-4926-403e-8158-6a449d006269","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
