{"parentUuid":"ee2223e9-ba09-4d8e-b261-f47da9ef4179","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ad490fb3266cc50f1","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffbubCj5uHAZuMw3ztVX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0163qXZJbHYanWJYUnHZ4MFQ","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve.md","old_string":"equals its change in momentum (∫F_net dt = mΔv). The sentences","new_string":"equals its change in momentum (∫F_net dt = mΔv, with Δv the change in velocity, not the book's ΔV). The sentences"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1680,"cache_read_input_tokens":127897,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1680,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_0163qXZJbHYanWJYUnHZ4MFQ":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve.md","old_string":"equals its change in momentum (∫F_net dt = mΔv). The sentences","new_string":"equals its change in momentum (∫F_net dt = mΔv, with Δv the change in velocity, not the book's ΔV). The sentences"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffbuRtPYy3BNsNtaVSaH","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"c63608c5-54b9-4975-ad74-2c7163135587","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:15:49.264Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"c63608c5-54b9-4975-ad74-2c7163135587","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"03e4fd49-e0c7-4b5d-bb77-f25bc8e8f726","agentId":"ad490fb3266cc50f1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0163qXZJbHYanWJYUnHZ4MFQ","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve.md has been updated successfully."}]},"uuid":"e2a4f069-cad0-4707-9be7-37dd24a31045","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:15:49.381Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve.md","oldString":"equals its change in momentum (∫F_net dt = mΔv). The sentences","newString":"equals its change in momentum (∫F_net dt = mΔv, with Δv the change in velocity, not the book's ΔV). The sentences","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.1\"\ntopic: impulse and momentum\ncategory: [biomechanics, force-time-curve, testing]\nconfidence: proven\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Newton's Second Law and the Impulse-Momentum Relation as the Mathematical Base of the Force-Time Curve, With the Book's Loose Wording Marked\n\n**What it is** — The authors open Chapter 5 by saying that if \"the Force-Time curve is to be regarded as the graphical starting point for sport biomechanics, then Newton's 2nd Law of Motion must logically serve as the corresponding mathematical starting point\" (PDF p. 266 / printed p. 255). They state Newton II as \"Force is proportional to the rate of change of momentum,\" write it as equation (5.1), F = d(m.v)/dt = m.dv/dt + v.dm/dt, and reduce it to F = m.a \"if the mass of the system is not changing with time,\" which they say holds in sport (PDF p. 266-267 / printed p. 255-256; equations verified against the page image). They then separate force \"generated ballistically or impulsively\" from force \"produced more slowly\": for slow force \"it is useful to refer to the momentum m.v of the body,\" but for \"brief transient force development, it is conventional to talk about the impulse I.\" The impulse is written I = ∫F.dt = ∫m.dv, or I = F.ΔT = m.ΔV, and \"Even if neither F nor ΔT is known, the impulse may be calculated from the change in momentum, m.ΔV\" (PDF p. 267 / printed p. 256, verified against the page image). Scope of `proven`: only the identity that the impulse of the net force on a body of constant mass equals its change in momentum (∫F_net dt = mΔv). The sentences listed in When NOT are outside that scope.\n\n**Why it works** — Inference: this is textbook mechanics. Integrating F_net = m.dv/dt over time gives ∫F_net dt = mΔv, so the area under a net-force-time curve equals the change in momentum whatever the curve's shape. That is why the take-off velocity of a jump fixes the net impulse even when the force record is not known, which is the authors' \"even if neither F nor ΔT is known\" point. The authors give no further mechanism; they use the relation to frame the RFD comparison of two athletes repeated from Ch 2.2.2 (Fig 5.1).\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: \"Force plates (CMJ testing) were already the department's longitudinal performance-tracking tool\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 73), and force-plate metrics are chosen by the ODSF framework, with \"1-3 driver metrics (what produces the output, e.g. braking/propulsive force)\" (line 79, adopted vendor framework). Inference: the impulse-momentum identity is what links a driver metric such as propulsive impulse to the output metric (take-off velocity, hence jump height), so it is the right lens when a jump changes and staff ask whether the athlete produced more impulse or the same impulse in a different way. Use the authors' split as a reading rule: for brief ballistic actions (take-off, throw release, strike) think in impulse over the short contact time; for slow efforts, momentum and peak force are adequate.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Four sentences in the passage are loose and should not be quoted as physics. (a) Inference: the v.dm/dt term in equation (5.1) is not a valid general form for a system that loses mass (the correct form uses the velocity of the ejected mass relative to the body), though the book's own conclusion F = m.a for constant mass, the sport case, is right. (b) Inference: \"I = F.ΔT\" is true only if F is read as the average force over ΔT; the book does not say so. (c) The authors write that the area under athlete B's curve in Fig 5.1 is \"(i.e. the momentum)\" (PDF p. 267 / printed p. 256). Inference: the area under a force-time curve is that force's impulse; it equals a body's change in momentum only when it is the net force on a free body. In Fig 5.1 the athletes perform a maximal effort and \"hold the load until fatigue forced them to stop,\" so no body is gaining that momentum; read the area as total impulse of effort, not momentum. (d) The authors write that \"any change in direction, and not only magnitude, will also be associated with a change in force\" (PDF p. 267 / printed p. 256). Inference: any change in the velocity vector, direction alone included, requires a net force; whether the force itself changes depends on the motion (constant-speed circular motion has a constant-size force that only turns). Do not use (c) or (d) as stated.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example is Fig 5.1 (verified against the page image): athlete A exerts more force than B at any instant before time T2, so A has the advantage where \"rapid RFD\" is needed (jumping, striking, throwing), while B reaches the higher peak and sustains force longer, an advantage in \"wrestling, powerlifting and scrummaging in rugby\" (PDF p. 267 / printed p. 256). This comparison is already atomized from Ch 2.2.2 in [[principle--force-time-profile-determines-strength-vs-power-training-target]].\n\n**Related** — [[principle--force-time-profile-determines-strength-vs-power-training-target]] (Fig 5.1 repeats the Ch 2 two-athlete comparison); [[heuristic--rfd-as-correspondence-criterion-and-impulse-cannot-compare-movements]] (4.3.4, the authors' caution on comparing impulse across movements); [[claim--average-or-peak-force-times-velocity-is-not-power]] (4.3.4, the companion warning on power); [[principle--rate-of-force-development-as-independent-limiter]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":13,"oldLines":7,"newStart":13,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," # Newton's Second Law and the Impulse-Momentum Relation as the Mathematical Base of the Force-Time Curve, With the Book's Loose Wording Marked"," ","-**What it is** — The authors open Chapter 5 by saying that if \"the Force-Time curve is to be regarded as the graphical starting point for sport biomechanics, then Newton's 2nd Law of Motion must logically serve as the corresponding mathematical starting point\" (PDF p. 266 / printed p. 255). They state Newton II as \"Force is proportional to the rate of change of momentum,\" write it as equation (5.1), F = d(m.v)/dt = m.dv/dt + v.dm/dt, and reduce it to F = m.a \"if the mass of the system is not changing with time,\" which they say holds in sport (PDF p. 266-267 / printed p. 255-256; equations verified against the page image). They then separate force \"generated ballistically or impulsively\" from force \"produced more slowly\": for slow force \"it is useful to refer to the momentum m.v of the body,\" but for \"brief transient force development, it is conventional to talk about the impulse I.\" The impulse is written I = ∫F.dt = ∫m.dv, or I = F.ΔT = m.ΔV, and \"Even if neither F nor ΔT is known, the impulse may be calculated from the change in momentum, m.ΔV\" (PDF p. 267 / printed p. 256, verified against the page image). Scope of `proven`: only the identity that the impulse of the net force on a body of constant mass equals its change in momentum (∫F_net dt = mΔv). The sentences listed in When NOT are outside that scope.","+**What it is** — The authors open Chapter 5 by saying that if \"the Force-Time curve is to be regarded as the graphical starting point for sport biomechanics, then Newton's 2nd Law of Motion must logically serve as the corresponding mathematical starting point\" (PDF p. 266 / printed p. 255). They state Newton II as \"Force is proportional to the rate of change of momentum,\" write it as equation (5.1), F = d(m.v)/dt = m.dv/dt + v.dm/dt, and reduce it to F = m.a \"if the mass of the system is not changing with time,\" which they say holds in sport (PDF p. 266-267 / printed p. 255-256; equations verified against the page image). They then separate force \"generated ballistically or impulsively\" from force \"produced more slowly\": for slow force \"it is useful to refer to the momentum m.v of the body,\" but for \"brief transient force development, it is conventional to talk about the impulse I.\" The impulse is written I = ∫F.dt = ∫m.dv, or I = F.ΔT = m.ΔV, and \"Even if neither F nor ΔT is known, the impulse may be calculated from the change in momentum, m.ΔV\" (PDF p. 267 / printed p. 256, verified against the page image). Scope of `proven`: only the identity that the impulse of the net force on a body of constant mass equals its change in momentum (∫F_net dt = mΔv, with Δv the change in velocity, not the book's ΔV). The sentences listed in When NOT are outside that scope."," "," **Why it works** — Inference: this is textbook mechanics. Integrating F_net = m.dv/dt over time gives ∫F_net dt = mΔv, so the area under a net-force-time curve equals the change in momentum whatever the curve's shape. That is why the take-off velocity of a jump fixes the net impulse even when the force record is not known, which is the authors' \"even if neither F nor ΔT is known\" point. The authors give no further mechanism; they use the relation to frame the RFD comparison of two athletes repeated from Ch 2.2.2 (Fig 5.1)."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false,"contentNotInModelContext":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c63608c5-54b9-4975-ad74-2c7163135587","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"e2a4f069-cad0-4707-9be7-37dd24a31045","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ad490fb3266cc50f1","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffbubCj5uHAZuMw3ztVX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CXiDsGqL2KChdaA2TXB4x5","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve.md","old_string":"That is why the take-off velocity of a jump fixes the net impulse even","new_string":"That is why, for a jump from a still start, the take-off velocity fixes the net impulse even"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1680,"cache_read_input_tokens":127897,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1680,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01CXiDsGqL2KChdaA2TXB4x5":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve.md","old_string":"That is why the take-off velocity of a jump fixes the net impulse even","new_string":"That is why, for a jump from a still start, the take-off velocity fixes the net impulse even"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffbuRtPYy3BNsNtaVSaH","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"8e36e57d-406d-46ca-9f0c-5923723c0f35","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:15:50.816Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"8e36e57d-406d-46ca-9f0c-5923723c0f35","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"03e4fd49-e0c7-4b5d-bb77-f25bc8e8f726","agentId":"ad490fb3266cc50f1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CXiDsGqL2KChdaA2TXB4x5","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve.md has been updated successfully."}]},"uuid":"b3be082d-8aa0-4c64-84f6-c6c09b08fb17","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:15:50.926Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve.md","oldString":"That is why the take-off velocity of a jump fixes the net impulse even","newString":"That is why, for a jump from a still start, the take-off velocity fixes the net impulse even","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.1\"\ntopic: impulse and momentum\ncategory: [biomechanics, force-time-curve, testing]\nconfidence: proven\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Newton's Second Law and the Impulse-Momentum Relation as the Mathematical Base of the Force-Time Curve, With the Book's Loose Wording Marked\n\n**What it is** — The authors open Chapter 5 by saying that if \"the Force-Time curve is to be regarded as the graphical starting point for sport biomechanics, then Newton's 2nd Law of Motion must logically serve as the corresponding mathematical starting point\" (PDF p. 266 / printed p. 255). They state Newton II as \"Force is proportional to the rate of change of momentum,\" write it as equation (5.1), F = d(m.v)/dt = m.dv/dt + v.dm/dt, and reduce it to F = m.a \"if the mass of the system is not changing with time,\" which they say holds in sport (PDF p. 266-267 / printed p. 255-256; equations verified against the page image). They then separate force \"generated ballistically or impulsively\" from force \"produced more slowly\": for slow force \"it is useful to refer to the momentum m.v of the body,\" but for \"brief transient force development, it is conventional to talk about the impulse I.\" The impulse is written I = ∫F.dt = ∫m.dv, or I = F.ΔT = m.ΔV, and \"Even if neither F nor ΔT is known, the impulse may be calculated from the change in momentum, m.ΔV\" (PDF p. 267 / printed p. 256, verified against the page image). Scope of `proven`: only the identity that the impulse of the net force on a body of constant mass equals its change in momentum (∫F_net dt = mΔv, with Δv the change in velocity, not the book's ΔV). The sentences listed in When NOT are outside that scope.\n\n**Why it works** — Inference: this is textbook mechanics. Integrating F_net = m.dv/dt over time gives ∫F_net dt = mΔv, so the area under a net-force-time curve equals the change in momentum whatever the curve's shape. That is why the take-off velocity of a jump fixes the net impulse even when the force record is not known, which is the authors' \"even if neither F nor ΔT is known\" point. The authors give no further mechanism; they use the relation to frame the RFD comparison of two athletes repeated from Ch 2.2.2 (Fig 5.1).\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: \"Force plates (CMJ testing) were already the department's longitudinal performance-tracking tool\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 73), and force-plate metrics are chosen by the ODSF framework, with \"1-3 driver metrics (what produces the output, e.g. braking/propulsive force)\" (line 79, adopted vendor framework). Inference: the impulse-momentum identity is what links a driver metric such as propulsive impulse to the output metric (take-off velocity, hence jump height), so it is the right lens when a jump changes and staff ask whether the athlete produced more impulse or the same impulse in a different way. Use the authors' split as a reading rule: for brief ballistic actions (take-off, throw release, strike) think in impulse over the short contact time; for slow efforts, momentum and peak force are adequate.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Four sentences in the passage are loose and should not be quoted as physics. (a) Inference: the v.dm/dt term in equation (5.1) is not a valid general form for a system that loses mass (the correct form uses the velocity of the ejected mass relative to the body), though the book's own conclusion F = m.a for constant mass, the sport case, is right. (b) Inference: \"I = F.ΔT\" is true only if F is read as the average force over ΔT; the book does not say so. (c) The authors write that the area under athlete B's curve in Fig 5.1 is \"(i.e. the momentum)\" (PDF p. 267 / printed p. 256). Inference: the area under a force-time curve is that force's impulse; it equals a body's change in momentum only when it is the net force on a free body. In Fig 5.1 the athletes perform a maximal effort and \"hold the load until fatigue forced them to stop,\" so no body is gaining that momentum; read the area as total impulse of effort, not momentum. (d) The authors write that \"any change in direction, and not only magnitude, will also be associated with a change in force\" (PDF p. 267 / printed p. 256). Inference: any change in the velocity vector, direction alone included, requires a net force; whether the force itself changes depends on the motion (constant-speed circular motion has a constant-size force that only turns). Do not use (c) or (d) as stated.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example is Fig 5.1 (verified against the page image): athlete A exerts more force than B at any instant before time T2, so A has the advantage where \"rapid RFD\" is needed (jumping, striking, throwing), while B reaches the higher peak and sustains force longer, an advantage in \"wrestling, powerlifting and scrummaging in rugby\" (PDF p. 267 / printed p. 256). This comparison is already atomized from Ch 2.2.2 in [[principle--force-time-profile-determines-strength-vs-power-training-target]].\n\n**Related** — [[principle--force-time-profile-determines-strength-vs-power-training-target]] (Fig 5.1 repeats the Ch 2 two-athlete comparison); [[heuristic--rfd-as-correspondence-criterion-and-impulse-cannot-compare-movements]] (4.3.4, the authors' caution on comparing impulse across movements); [[claim--average-or-peak-force-times-velocity-is-not-power]] (4.3.4, the companion warning on power); [[principle--rate-of-force-development-as-independent-limiter]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":15,"oldLines":7,"newStart":15,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **What it is** — The authors open Chapter 5 by saying that if \"the Force-Time curve is to be regarded as the graphical starting point for sport biomechanics, then Newton's 2nd Law of Motion must logically serve as the corresponding mathematical starting point\" (PDF p. 266 / printed p. 255). They state Newton II as \"Force is proportional to the rate of change of momentum,\" write it as equation (5.1), F = d(m.v)/dt = m.dv/dt + v.dm/dt, and reduce it to F = m.a \"if the mass of the system is not changing with time,\" which they say holds in sport (PDF p. 266-267 / printed p. 255-256; equations verified against the page image). They then separate force \"generated ballistically or impulsively\" from force \"produced more slowly\": for slow force \"it is useful to refer to the momentum m.v of the body,\" but for \"brief transient force development, it is conventional to talk about the impulse I.\" The impulse is written I = ∫F.dt = ∫m.dv, or I = F.ΔT = m.ΔV, and \"Even if neither F nor ΔT is known, the impulse may be calculated from the change in momentum, m.ΔV\" (PDF p. 267 / printed p. 256, verified against the page image). Scope of `proven`: only the identity that the impulse of the net force on a body of constant mass equals its change in momentum (∫F_net dt = mΔv, with Δv the change in velocity, not the book's ΔV). The sentences listed in When NOT are outside that scope."," ","-**Why it works** — Inference: this is textbook mechanics. Integrating F_net = m.dv/dt over time gives ∫F_net dt = mΔv, so the area under a net-force-time curve equals the change in momentum whatever the curve's shape. That is why the take-off velocity of a jump fixes the net impulse even when the force record is not known, which is the authors' \"even if neither F nor ΔT is known\" point. The authors give no further mechanism; they use the relation to frame the RFD comparison of two athletes repeated from Ch 2.2.2 (Fig 5.1).","+**Why it works** — Inference: this is textbook mechanics. Integrating F_net = m.dv/dt over time gives ∫F_net dt = mΔv, so the area under a net-force-time curve equals the change in momentum whatever the curve's shape. That is why, for a jump from a still start, the take-off velocity fixes the net impulse even when the force record is not known, which is the authors' \"even if neither F nor ΔT is known\" point. The authors give no further mechanism; they use the relation to frame the RFD comparison of two athletes repeated from Ch 2.2.2 (Fig 5.1)."," "," **When to apply** — Eric's baseline: \"Force plates (CMJ testing) were already the department's longitudinal performance-tracking tool\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 73), and force-plate metrics are chosen by the ODSF framework, with \"1-3 driver metrics (what produces the output, e.g. braking/propulsive force)\" (line 79, adopted vendor framework). Inference: the impulse-momentum identity is what links a driver metric such as propulsive impulse to the output metric (take-off velocity, hence jump height), so it is the right lens when a jump changes and staff ask whether the athlete produced more impulse or the same impulse in a different way. Use the authors' split as a reading rule: for brief ballistic actions (take-off, throw release, strike) think in impulse over the short contact time; for slow efforts, momentum and peak force are adequate."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false,"contentNotInModelContext":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"8e36e57d-406d-46ca-9f0c-5923723c0f35","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"b3be082d-8aa0-4c64-84f6-c6c09b08fb17","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ad490fb3266cc50f1","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffbubCj5uHAZuMw3ztVX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TzwKoyBBCeGDCY4QAhKhzq","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve.md","old_string":"a driver metric such as propulsive impulse to the output metric","new_string":"a driver metric such as net propulsive impulse (vertical ground reaction force minus body weight, integrated over the propulsive phase, which starts at zero vertical velocity at the bottom of the countermovement) to the output metric"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1680,"cache_read_input_tokens":127897,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1680,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01TzwKoyBBCeGDCY4QAhKhzq":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve.md","old_string":"a driver metric such as propulsive impulse to the output metric","new_string":"a driver metric such as net propulsive impulse (vertical ground reaction force minus body weight, integrated over the propulsive phase, which starts at zero vertical velocity at the bottom of the countermovement) to the output metric"}},"apiBlockIndex":3,"requestId":"req_011CffbuRtPYy3BNsNtaVSaH","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"f5d5d26e-76fe-4cc7