{"parentUuid":"7ecfed3a-2ffb-4656-ad09-37f961611518","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ad490fb3266cc50f1","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffbvgaD7s2vtj7Mmum8j","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2486,"cache_read_input_tokens":129577,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2486,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":586,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffbvZW4mc2b9aDjnX5p5","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"3db4c4db-43a5-4f36-8d37-f243897b3ea4","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:16:01.882Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"3db4c4db-43a5-4f36-8d37-f243897b3ea4","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ad490fb3266cc50f1","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffbvgaD7s2vtj7Mmum8j","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011pHvqdY8kuGpoxZmxc1Gdt","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve.md","old_string":"requires a net force; the force itself need not change: a projectile","new_string":"requires a net force, but the force itself need not change. A projectile"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2486,"cache_read_input_tokens":129577,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2486,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_011pHvqdY8kuGpoxZmxc1Gdt":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve.md","old_string":"requires a net force; the force itself need not change: a projectile","new_string":"requires a net force, but the force itself need not change. A projectile"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffbvZW4mc2b9aDjnX5p5","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"cf089806-f895-46f3-9186-2cd3d7098b32","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:16:03.027Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"cf089806-f895-46f3-9186-2cd3d7098b32","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"03e4fd49-e0c7-4b5d-bb77-f25bc8e8f726","agentId":"ad490fb3266cc50f1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011pHvqdY8kuGpoxZmxc1Gdt","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve.md has been updated successfully."}]},"uuid":"3bd0d0e5-edf8-4a23-847f-b7a62d125962","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:16:03.127Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve.md","oldString":"requires a net force; the force itself need not change: a projectile","newString":"requires a net force, but the force itself need not change. A projectile","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.1\"\ntopic: impulse and momentum\ncategory: [biomechanics, force-time-curve, testing]\nconfidence: proven\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Newton's Second Law and the Impulse-Momentum Relation as the Mathematical Base of the Force-Time Curve, With the Book's Loose Wording Marked\n\n**What it is** — The authors open Chapter 5 by saying that if \"the Force-Time curve is to be regarded as the graphical starting point for sport biomechanics, then Newton's 2nd Law of Motion must logically serve as the corresponding mathematical starting point\" (PDF p. 266 / printed p. 255). They state Newton II as \"Force is proportional to the rate of change of momentum,\" write it as equation (5.1), F = d(m.v)/dt = m.dv/dt + v.dm/dt, and reduce it to F = m.a \"if the mass of the system is not changing with time,\" which they say holds in sport (PDF p. 266-267 / printed p. 255-256; equations verified against the page image). They then separate force \"generated ballistically or impulsively\" from force \"produced more slowly\": for slow force \"it is useful to refer to the momentum m.v of the body,\" but for \"brief transient force development, it is conventional to talk about the impulse I.\" The impulse is written I = ∫F.dt = ∫m.dv, or I = F.ΔT = m.ΔV, and \"Even if neither F nor ΔT is known, the impulse may be calculated from the change in momentum, m.ΔV\" (PDF p. 267 / printed p. 256, verified against the page image). Scope of `proven`: only the identity that the impulse of the net force on a body of constant mass equals its change in momentum (∫F_net dt = mΔv, with Δv the change in velocity, not the book's ΔV). The sentences listed in When NOT are outside that scope.\n\n**Why it works** — Inference: this is textbook mechanics. Integrating F_net = m.dv/dt over time gives ∫F_net dt = mΔv, so the area under a net-force-time curve equals the change in momentum whatever the curve's shape. That is why, for a jump from a still start, the take-off velocity fixes the net impulse even when the force record is not known, which is the authors' \"even if neither F nor ΔT is known\" point. The authors give no further mechanism; they use the relation to frame the RFD comparison of two athletes repeated from Ch 2.2.2 (Fig 5.1).\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: \"Force plates (CMJ testing) were already the department's longitudinal performance-tracking tool\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 73), and force-plate metrics are chosen by the ODSF framework, with \"1-3 driver metrics (what produces the output, e.g. braking/propulsive force)\" (line 79, adopted vendor framework). Inference: the impulse-momentum identity is what links a driver metric such as net propulsive impulse (vertical ground reaction force minus body weight, integrated over the propulsive phase, which starts at zero vertical velocity at the bottom of the countermovement) to the output metric (take-off velocity, hence jump height), so it is the right lens when a jump changes and staff ask whether the athlete produced more impulse or the same impulse in a different way. Use the authors' split as a reading rule: for brief ballistic actions (take-off, throw release, strike) think in impulse over the short contact time; for slow efforts, momentum and peak force are adequate.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Five sentences in the passage are loose and should not be quoted as physics. (a) Inference: the v.dm/dt term in equation (5.1) is not a valid general form for a system that loses mass (the correct form uses the velocity of the ejected mass relative to the body), though the book's own conclusion F = m.a for constant mass, the sport case, is right. (b) Inference: \"I = F.ΔT\" is true only if F is read as the average force over ΔT; the book does not say so. (c) The authors write that the area under athlete B's curve in Fig 5.1 is \"(i.e. the momentum)\" (PDF p. 267 / printed p. 256). Inference: the area under a force-time curve is that force's impulse; it equals a body's change in momentum only when it is the net external force on the body. In Fig 5.1 the athletes perform a maximal effort and \"hold the load until fatigue forced them to stop,\" so no body is gaining that momentum; read the area as total impulse of effort, not momentum. (d) The authors write that \"any change in direction, and not only magnitude, will also be associated with a change in force\" (PDF p. 267 / printed p. 256). Inference: any change in the velocity vector, direction alone included, requires a net force; the force itself need not change: a projectile under gravity (ignoring air resistance) changes direction continuously while the force on it stays constant, so a change in velocity direction does not require a change in force, only a net force. (e) The authors define ΔV as \"The final velocity reached at the end of this phase\" and integrate from 0 to ΔV (PDF p. 267 / printed p. 256, verified against the page image). Inference: so m.ΔV equals the change in momentum only when the body starts from rest; in general the impulse of the net external force equals m(v_final - v_initial). Do not use (c), (d) or (e) as stated.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example is Fig 5.1 (verified against the page image): athlete A exerts more force than B at any instant before time T2, so A has the advantage where \"rapid RFD\" is needed (jumping, striking, throwing), while B reaches the higher peak and sustains force longer, an advantage in \"wrestling, powerlifting and scrummaging in rugby\" (PDF p. 267 / printed p. 256). This comparison is already atomized from Ch 2.2.2 in [[principle--force-time-profile-determines-strength-vs-power-training-target]].\n\n**Related** — [[principle--force-time-profile-determines-strength-vs-power-training-target]] (Fig 5.1 repeats the Ch 2 two-athlete comparison); [[heuristic--rfd-as-correspondence-criterion-and-impulse-cannot-compare-movements]] (4.3.4, the authors' caution on comparing impulse across movements); [[claim--average-or-peak-force-times-velocity-is-not-power]] (4.3.4, the companion warning on power); [[principle--rate-of-force-development-as-independent-limiter]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":19,"oldLines":7,"newStart":19,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — Eric's baseline: \"Force plates (CMJ testing) were already the department's longitudinal performance-tracking tool\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 73), and force-plate metrics are chosen by the ODSF framework, with \"1-3 driver metrics (what produces the output, e.g. braking/propulsive force)\" (line 79, adopted vendor framework). Inference: the impulse-momentum identity is what links a driver metric such as net propulsive impulse (vertical ground reaction force minus body weight, integrated over the propulsive phase, which starts at zero vertical velocity at the bottom of the countermovement) to the output metric (take-off velocity, hence jump height), so it is the right lens when a jump changes and staff ask whether the athlete produced more impulse or the same impulse in a different way. Use the authors' split as a reading rule: for brief ballistic actions (take-off, throw release, strike) think in impulse over the short contact time; for slow efforts, momentum and peak force are adequate."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — Five sentences in the passage are loose and should not be quoted as physics. (a) Inference: the v.dm/dt term in equation (5.1) is not a valid general form for a system that loses mass (the correct form uses the velocity of the ejected mass relative to the body), though the book's own conclusion F = m.a for constant mass, the sport case, is right. (b) Inference: \"I = F.ΔT\" is true only if F is read as the average force over ΔT; the book does not say so. (c) The authors write that the area under athlete B's curve in Fig 5.1 is \"(i.e. the momentum)\" (PDF p. 267 / printed p. 256). Inference: the area under a force-time curve is that force's impulse; it equals a body's change in momentum only when it is the net external force on the body. In Fig 5.1 the athletes perform a maximal effort and \"hold the load until fatigue forced them to stop,\" so no body is gaining that momentum; read the area as total impulse of effort, not momentum. (d) The authors write that \"any change in direction, and not only magnitude, will also be associated with a change in force\" (PDF p. 267 / printed p. 256). Inference: any change in the velocity vector, direction alone included, requires a net force; the force itself need not change: a projectile under gravity (ignoring air resistance) changes direction continuously while the force on it stays constant, so a change in velocity direction does not require a change in force, only a net force. (e) The authors define ΔV as \"The final velocity reached at the end of this phase\" and integrate from 0 to ΔV (PDF p. 267 / printed p. 256, verified against the page image). Inference: so m.ΔV equals the change in momentum only when the body starts from rest; in general the impulse of the net external force equals m(v_final - v_initial). Do not use (c), (d) or (e) as stated.","+**When NOT to apply** — Five sentences in the passage are loose and should not be quoted as physics. (a) Inference: the v.dm/dt term in equation (5.1) is not a valid general form for a system that loses mass (the correct form uses the velocity of the ejected mass relative to the body), though the book's own conclusion F = m.a for constant mass, the sport case, is right. (b) Inference: \"I = F.ΔT\" is true only if F is read as the average force over ΔT; the book does not say so. (c) The authors write that the area under athlete B's curve in Fig 5.1 is \"(i.e. the momentum)\" (PDF p. 267 / printed p. 256). Inference: the area under a force-time curve is that force's impulse; it equals a body's change in momentum only when it is the net external force on the body. In Fig 5.1 the athletes perform a maximal effort and \"hold the load until fatigue forced them to stop,\" so no body is gaining that momentum; read the area as total impulse of effort, not momentum. (d) The authors write that \"any change in direction, and not only magnitude, will also be associated with a change in force\" (PDF p. 267 / printed p. 256). Inference: any change in the velocity vector, direction alone included, requires a net force, but the force itself need not change. A projectile under gravity (ignoring air resistance) changes direction continuously while the force on it stays constant, so a change in velocity direction does not require a change in force, only a net force. (e) The authors define ΔV as \"The final velocity reached at the end of this phase\" and integrate from 0 to ΔV (PDF p. 267 / printed p. 256, verified against the page image). Inference: so m.ΔV equals the change in momentum only when the body starts from rest; in general the impulse of the net external force equals m(v_final - v_initial). Do not use (c), (d) or (e) as stated."," "," **Example** — The authors' own example is Fig 5.1 (verified against the page image): athlete A exerts more force than B at any instant before time T2, so A has the advantage where \"rapid RFD\" is needed (jumping, striking, throwing), while B reaches the higher peak and sustains force longer, an advantage in \"wrestling, powerlifting and scrummaging in rugby\" (PDF p. 267 / printed p. 256). This comparison is already atomized from Ch 2.2.2 in [[principle--force-time-profile-determines-strength-vs-power-training-target]]."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false,"contentNotInModelContext":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"cf089806-f895-46f3-9186-2cd3d7098b32","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"3bd0d0e5-edf8-4a23-847f-b7a62d125962","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ad490fb3266cc50f1","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffbvgaD7s2vtj7Mmum8j","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01X3v5jUJDuA4TN7q7KDFe3t","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--dynamic-correspondence-means-matching-motor-characteristics-not-copying-the-sport-movement.md","offset":16,"limit":5},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2486,"cache_read_input_tokens":129577,"output_tokens":490,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":100},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2486},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":490,"cache_read_input_tokens":129577,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2486,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2486,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01X3v5jUJDuA4TN7q7KDFe3t":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--dynamic-correspondence-means-matching-motor-characteristics-not-copying-the-sport-movement.md","offset":16,"limit":5}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffbvZW4mc2b9aDjnX5p5","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"1d179231-40e2-4fbe-84ab-a49e8a26253d","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:16:04.162Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"1d179231-40e2-4fbe-84ab-a49e8a26253d","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"03e4fd49-e0c7-4b5d-bb77-f25bc8e8f726","agentId":"ad490fb3266cc50f1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01X3v5jUJDuA4TN7q7KDFe3t","type":"tool_result","content":"16\t**What it is** — The authors warn of \"an excessive passion for making the strength training means correspond structurally to the special exercises.\" The misinterpretation of dynamic correspondence \"lies in literally copying the special exercise in training movements, instead of selecting a specific arsenal of means corresponding to the most important motor characteristics of the given sport and controlling their growth by creating conditions under which they will develop steadily.\" In their words, \"it is inappropriate to try to simulate sports specific movements with supplementary training activities,\" because this \"not only does ... not accurately develop the necessary fitness and motor abilities, but it can also alter the neuromuscular programmes which control the motor actions.\" The aim is \"the specific type of fitness and motor characteristics of the sport\" (PDF p. 268 / printed p. 257, verified against the page image). The paragraph before 5.1's dynamics discussion sets this up: the shape of the force-time curve depends on the neuromuscular system's ability to develop force \"with the speed necessary to produce the required motor effect,\" which \"requires specialised means of training,\" and the authors go as far as \"All subsequent training and performance errors are caused by inappropriate neuromuscular programming\" (PDF p. 267-268 / printed p. 256-257).\n17\t\n18\t**Why it works** — The authors' reason is that a loaded copy of a skill changes the skill's neuromuscular programme while missing the motor characteristic that matters. They give no further mechanism or data on these pages. Inference: the five criteria in 4.3 (amplitude and direction, accentuated region, dynamics of effort, rate and time of force, regime of work) are the tool for picking a \"specific arsenal of means\" by characteristic rather than by look.\n19\t\n20\t**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: the Principle of Dynamic Correspondence \"governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 103, Explicit, Q1). This passage is the authors' own statement of that \"not surface resemblance\" rule. Use it when a coach asks for a loaded version of a sport skill (a weighted throwing motion, a banded route stem, a loaded sprint drill that changes the mechanics) and the question is whether it trains the quality or only resembles the skill."}]},"uuid":"74460b80-e56e-4eba-b8e3-6baa7bf5e80c","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:16:04.316Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--dynamic-correspondence-means-matching-motor-characteristics-not-copying-the-sport-movement.md","content":"**What it is** — The authors warn of \"an excessive passion for making the strength training means correspond structurally to the special exercises.\" The misinterpretation of dynamic correspondence \"lies in literally copying the special exercise in training movements, instead of selecting a specific arsenal of means corresponding to the most important motor characteristics of the given sport and controlling their growth by creating conditions under which they will develop steadily.\" In their words, \"it is inappropriate to try to simulate sports specific movements with supplementary training activities,\" because this \"not only does ... not accurately develop the necessary fitness and motor abilities, but it can also alter the neuromuscular programmes which control the motor actions.\" The aim is \"the specific type of fitness and motor characteristics of the sport\" (PDF p. 268 / printed p. 257, verified against the page image). The paragraph before 5.1's dynamics discussion sets this up: the shape of the force-time curve depends on the neuromuscular system's ability to develop force \"with the speed necessary to produce the required motor effect,\" which \"requires specialised means of training,\" and the authors go as far as \"All subsequent training and performance errors are caused by inappropriate neuromuscular programming\" (PDF p. 267-268 / printed p. 256-257).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is that a loaded copy of a skill changes the skill's neuromuscular programme while missing the motor characteristic that matters. They give no further mechanism or data on these pages. Inference: the five criteria in 4.3 (amplitude and direction, accentuated region, dynamics of effort, rate and time of force, regime of work) are the tool for picking a \"specific arsenal of means\" by characteristic rather than by look.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: the Principle of Dynamic Correspondence \"governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 103, Explicit, Q1). This passage is the authors' own statement of that \"not surface resemblance\" rule. Use it when a coach asks for a loaded version of a sport skill (a weighted throwing motion, a banded route stem, a loaded sprint drill that changes the mechanics) and the question is whether it trains the quality or only resembles the skill.","numLines":5,"startLine":16,"totalLines":27}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"1d179231-40e2-4fbe-84ab-a49e8a26253d","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
