{"parentUuid":"0d4e85c5-0a32-4291-bb87-8ee8a0a1cc79","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ad490fb3266cc50f1","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffbwRT9Jz8H3YCnQyKMq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MGVkJCS2XdL6kFq5SVDcEE","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--dynamic-correspondence-means-matching-motor-characteristics-not-copying-the-sport-movement.md","old_string":"The paragraph before 5.1's dynamics discussion sets this up:","new_string":"The paragraph that closes 5.1's dynamics discussion, after Fig 5.1, sets this up:"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1415,"cache_read_input_tokens":132063,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1415,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01MGVkJCS2XdL6kFq5SVDcEE":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--dynamic-correspondence-means-matching-motor-characteristics-not-copying-the-sport-movement.md","old_string":"The paragraph before 5.1's dynamics discussion sets this up:","new_string":"The paragraph that closes 5.1's dynamics discussion, after Fig 5.1, sets this up:"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cffbw1E88YjgzLH4vFHNj","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"0e1028a9-a949-4bb1-889a-cb18af64c23f","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:16:12.431Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"0e1028a9-a949-4bb1-889a-cb18af64c23f","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"03e4fd49-e0c7-4b5d-bb77-f25bc8e8f726","agentId":"ad490fb3266cc50f1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MGVkJCS2XdL6kFq5SVDcEE","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--dynamic-correspondence-means-matching-motor-characteristics-not-copying-the-sport-movement.md has been updated successfully."}]},"uuid":"0d262ac4-ada7-45ad-8e88-415ec78e917c","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:16:12.531Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--dynamic-correspondence-means-matching-motor-characteristics-not-copying-the-sport-movement.md","oldString":"The paragraph before 5.1's dynamics discussion sets this up:","newString":"The paragraph that closes 5.1's dynamics discussion, after Fig 5.1, sets this up:","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.1\"\ntopic: misreading dynamic correspondence\ncategory: [dynamic-correspondence, exercise-selection, transfer]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Dynamic Correspondence Means Matching the Sport's Motor Characteristics, Not Copying the Sport Movement Under Load\n\n**What it is** — The authors warn of \"an excessive passion for making the strength training means correspond structurally to the special exercises.\" The misinterpretation of dynamic correspondence \"lies in literally copying the special exercise in training movements, instead of selecting a specific arsenal of means corresponding to the most important motor characteristics of the given sport and controlling their growth by creating conditions under which they will develop steadily.\" In their words, \"it is inappropriate to try to simulate sports specific movements with supplementary training activities,\" because this \"not only does ... not accurately develop the necessary fitness and motor abilities, but it can also alter the neuromuscular programmes which control the motor actions.\" The aim is \"the specific type of fitness and motor characteristics of the sport\" (PDF p. 268 / printed p. 257, verified against the page image). The paragraph before 5.1's dynamics discussion sets this up: the shape of the force-time curve depends on the neuromuscular system's ability to develop force \"with the speed necessary to produce the required motor effect,\" which \"requires specialised means of training,\" and the authors go as far as \"All subsequent training and performance errors are caused by inappropriate neuromuscular programming\" (PDF p. 267-268 / printed p. 256-257).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is that a loaded copy of a skill changes the skill's neuromuscular programme while missing the motor characteristic that matters. They give no further mechanism or data on these pages. Inference: the five criteria in 4.3 (amplitude and direction, accentuated region, dynamics of effort, rate and time of force, regime of work) are the tool for picking a \"specific arsenal of means\" by characteristic rather than by look.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: the Principle of Dynamic Correspondence \"governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 103, Explicit, Q1). This passage is the authors' own statement of that \"not surface resemblance\" rule. Use it when a coach asks for a loaded version of a sport skill (a weighted throwing motion, a banded route stem, a loaded sprint drill that changes the mechanics) and the question is whether it trains the quality or only resembles the skill.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors do not ban all resisted sport movements: in 4.3 they discuss parachute resistance for sprinters (see [[claim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence]]), and in 5.2.3 they write that \"good results are achieved by making the special exercise more demanding,\" with water resistance in rowing or very small limb loads enhancing speed-strength \"under conditions that are very close to those of the special exercise\" (PDF p. 277-278 / printed p. 266-267). Inference: the line is whether the load changes the movement's structure; light loads that leave the pattern intact fall outside this warning. The sweeping sentence \"All subsequent training and performance errors are caused by inappropriate neuromuscular programming\" is an assertion without evidence on the page; do not quote it as a finding.\n\n**Example** — Inference: the source gives no named example here. A heavy cable \"simulated\" football block that slows and reshapes the punch would be the literal copy the authors warn against; a heavy push or pull that matches the punch's accentuated region and rate of force, plus fast unloaded punches, would be the \"arsenal of means\" approach.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]]; [[heuristic--train-coupled-hip-flexion-extension-without-heavily-loaded-simulation]]; [[claim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence]]; [[principle--specificity-of-training-ten-dimensions]]; [[elite-sports-performance--evans/case-study--dynamic-correspondence-applied-to-wrestling-takedown]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":13,"oldLines":7,"newStart":13,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," # Dynamic Correspondence Means Matching the Sport's Motor Characteristics, Not Copying the Sport Movement Under Load"," ","-**What it is** — The authors warn of \"an excessive passion for making the strength training means correspond structurally to the special exercises.\" The misinterpretation of dynamic correspondence \"lies in literally copying the special exercise in training movements, instead of selecting a specific arsenal of means corresponding to the most important motor characteristics of the given sport and controlling their growth by creating conditions under which they will develop steadily.\" In their words, \"it is inappropriate to try to simulate sports specific movements with supplementary training activities,\" because this \"not only does ... not accurately develop the necessary fitness and motor abilities, but it can also alter the neuromuscular programmes which control the motor actions.\" The aim is \"the specific type of fitness and motor characteristics of the sport\" (PDF p. 268 / printed p. 257, verified against the page image). The paragraph before 5.1's dynamics discussion sets this up: the shape of the force-time curve depends on the neuromuscular system's ability to develop force \"with the speed necessary to produce the required motor effect,\" which \"requires specialised means of training,\" and the authors go as far as \"All subsequent training and performance errors are caused by inappropriate neuromuscular programming\" (PDF p. 267-268 / printed p. 256-257).","+**What it is** — The authors warn of \"an excessive passion for making the strength training means correspond structurally to the special exercises.\" The misinterpretation of dynamic correspondence \"lies in literally copying the special exercise in training movements, instead of selecting a specific arsenal of means corresponding to the most important motor characteristics of the given sport and controlling their growth by creating conditions under which they will develop steadily.\" In their words, \"it is inappropriate to try to simulate sports specific movements with supplementary training activities,\" because this \"not only does ... not accurately develop the necessary fitness and motor abilities, but it can also alter the neuromuscular programmes which control the motor actions.\" The aim is \"the specific type of fitness and motor characteristics of the sport\" (PDF p. 268 / printed p. 257, verified against the page image). The paragraph that closes 5.1's dynamics discussion, after Fig 5.1, sets this up: the shape of the force-time curve depends on the neuromuscular system's ability to develop force \"with the speed necessary to produce the required motor effect,\" which \"requires specialised means of training,\" and the authors go as far as \"All subsequent training and performance errors are caused by inappropriate neuromuscular programming\" (PDF p. 267-268 / printed p. 256-257)."," "," **Why it works** — The authors' reason is that a loaded copy of a skill changes the skill's neuromuscular programme while missing the motor characteristic that matters. They give no further mechanism or data on these pages. Inference: the five criteria in 4.3 (amplitude and direction, accentuated region, dynamics of effort, rate and time of force, regime of work) are the tool for picking a \"specific arsenal of means\" by characteristic rather than by look."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false,"contentNotInModelContext":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"0e1028a9-a949-4bb1-889a-cb18af64c23f","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
