ports specific movements with supplementary training activities,\" because this \"not only does ... not accurately develop the necessary fitness and motor abilities, but it can also alter the neuromuscular programmes which control the motor actions.\" The aim is \"the specific type of fitness and motor characteristics of the sport\" (PDF p. 268 / printed p. 257, verified against the page image). The paragraph that closes 5.1's dynamics discussion, after Fig 5.1, sets this up: the shape of the force-time curve depends on the neuromuscular system's ability to develop force \"with the speed necessary to produce the required motor effect,\" which \"requires specialised means of training,\" and the authors go as far as \"All subsequent training and performance errors are caused by inappropriate neuromuscular programming\" (PDF p. 267-268 / printed p. 256-257).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is that a loaded copy of a skill changes the skill's neuromuscular programme while missing the motor characteristic that matters. They give no further mechanism or data on these pages. Inference: the five criteria in 4.3 (amplitude and direction, accentuated region, dynamics of effort, rate and time of force, regime of work) are the tool for picking a \"specific arsenal of means\" by characteristic rather than by look.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: the Principle of Dynamic Correspondence \"governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 103, Explicit, Q1). This passage is the authors' own statement of that \"not surface resemblance\" rule. Use it when a coach asks for a loaded version of a sport skill (a weighted throwing motion, a banded route stem, a loaded sprint drill that changes the mechanics) and the question is whether it trains the quality or only resembles the skill.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors do not ban all resisted sport movements: in 4.3 they discuss parachute resistance for sprinters (see [[claim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence]]), and in 5.2.3 they write that \"good results are achieved by making the special exercise more demanding,\" with water resistance in rowing or very small limb loads enhancing speed-strength \"under conditions that are very close to those of the special exercise\" (PDF p. 277-278 / printed p. 266-267). Inference: the line is whether the load changes the movement's structure; light loads that leave the pattern intact fall outside this warning. The sweeping sentence \"All subsequent training and performance errors are caused by inappropriate neuromuscular programming\" is an assertion without evidence on the page; do not quote it as a finding.\n\n**Example** — Inference: the source gives no named example here. A heavy cable \"simulated\" football block that slows and reshapes the punch would be the literal copy the authors warn against; a heavy push or pull that matches the punch's accentuated region and rate of force, plus fast unloaded punches, would be the \"arsenal of means\" approach.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]]; [[heuristic--train-coupled-hip-flexion-extension-without-heavily-loaded-simulation]]; [[claim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence]]; [[principle--specificity-of-training-ten-dimensions]]; [[elite-sports-performance--evans/case-study--dynamic-correspondence-applied-to-wrestling-takedown]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":17,"oldLines":7,"newStart":17,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **Why it works** — The authors' reason is that a loaded copy of a skill changes the skill's neuromuscular programme while missing the motor characteristic that matters. They give no further mechanism or data on these pages. Inference: the five criteria in 4.3 (amplitude and direction, accentuated region, dynamics of effort, rate and time of force, regime of work) are the tool for picking a \"specific arsenal of means\" by characteristic rather than by look."," ","-**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: the Principle of Dynamic Correspondence \"governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 103, Explicit, Q1). This passage is the authors' own statement of that \"not surface resemblance\" rule. Use it when a coach asks for a loaded version of a sport skill (a weighted throwing motion, a banded route stem, a loaded sprint drill that changes the mechanics) and the question is whether it trains the quality or only resembles the skill.","+**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: the Principle of Dynamic Correspondence \"governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 103, Explicit, Q1). Inference: this passage is the authors' own statement of that \"not surface resemblance\" rule. Use it when a coach asks for a loaded version of a sport skill (a weighted throwing motion, a banded route stem, a loaded sprint drill that changes the mechanics) and the question is whether it trains the quality or only resembles the skill."," "," **When NOT to apply** — The authors do not ban all resisted sport movements: in 4.3 they discuss parachute resistance for sprinters (see [[claim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence]]), and in 5.2.3 they write that \"good results are achieved by making the special exercise more demanding,\" with water resistance in rowing or very small limb loads enhancing speed-strength \"under conditions that are very close to those of the special exercise\" (PDF p. 277-278 / printed p. 266-267). Inference: the line is whether the load changes the movement's structure; light loads that leave the pattern intact fall outside this warning. The sweeping sentence \"All subsequent training and performance errors are caused by inappropriate neuromuscular programming\" is an assertion without evidence on the page; do not quote it as a finding."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false,"contentNotInModelContext":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"aed625e0-8f6d-403c-8653-b1f72e99b978","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"79f36269-6374-4403-9770-5e89d8632d34","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ad490fb3266cc50f1","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffbwRT9Jz8H3YCnQyKMq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KUv6xbustocaSG71HDmi78","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric.md","offset":23,"limit":7},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1415,"cache_read_input_tokens":132063,"output_tokens":629,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1415},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":629,"cache_read_input_tokens":132063,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1415,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1415,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01KUv6xbustocaSG71HDmi78":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric.md","offset":23,"limit":7}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011Cffbw1E88YjgzLH4vFHNj","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"bc3b635b-b1af-4d9a-a294-c20af1613613","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:16:14.884Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"bc3b635b-b1af-4d9a-a294-c20af1613613","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"03e4fd49-e0c7-4b5d-bb77-f25bc8e8f726","agentId":"ad490fb3266cc50f1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KUv6xbustocaSG71HDmi78","type":"tool_result","content":"23\t**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: \"No sport performs true 1RM testing. Maxes are projected from observation plus the athlete's history\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 61, Explicit, Q10), and %1RM prescription is kept \"stated explicitly as built on projected maxes\" (line 260, Tension 16). Inference: the percentage bands here are therefore percentages of a projected max, so the RM descriptions (3-5RM, 8-12 reps) are the safer way to apply them. The baseline's off-season rule (\"relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work,\" line 105, course material, Inferred). Inference: that rule lines up with the authors' sequence, repetitive effort first and brief maximal tension once it stops working. Inference: the in-season rule \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" (line 105) matches the authors' use of brief maximal tension \"for maintaining the existing level of strength.\"\n24\t\n25\t**When NOT to apply** — The book contradicts itself on several details; keep both sides. (1) Sets: brief maximal tension needs sets \"increased to more than three (Berger, 1962)\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260), but its effect \"is enhanced by increasing the maximal weight, increasing the average weight lifted per session and reducing the number of sets and repetitions\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). (2) Size of effect: brief maximal tension \"yields a larger training effect than the progressive resistance method for developing maximum strength\" (Diachkov 1961; Chudinov 1961; PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260), against \"Similar significant increases in maximum strength can be obtained by the repetitive effort and brief maximal tension methods, as well as with isometric tension\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). (3) Isometrics: isometric training \"does not increase strength significantly more\" than dynamic lifting (Salter 1955; PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260) and dynamic 3 x 5-6 \"is more effective than the corresponding amount of isometric work\" (Rasch & Morehouse 1957; Berger 1962; PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261), against the \"similar significant increases ... with isometric tension\" sentence on the same page. (4) Load-rep pairings: the 5RM is \"about 89%\" of 1RM (PDF p. 269 / printed p. 258), yet \"90-95% of 1RM\" is paired with \"3-6\" reps (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261), and \"85-95% of 1RM\" with \"3-5RM\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260). These three are not consistent; do not use any one as a rep-to-load conversion. Inference: for sports decided by fast force production, these three methods are not the whole answer; the authors put speed-strength and explosive work in separate method groups (5.2.3-5.2.4).\n26\t\n27\t**Example** — The authors' own prescription for weightlifters under brief maximal tension: \"5-6 exercises for 6-10 sets of 1-3 repetitions in one training session (Medvedev, 1967; Vorobyev, 1971)\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260).\n28\t\n29\t**Related** — [[claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared]]; [[claim--berger-sets-reps-and-frequency-studies-in-novices]]; [[claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves]]; [[claim--strength-detraining-and-retention-timelines]]; [[framework--types-of-maximal-strength]]; [[framework--sarcomere-vs-sarcoplasmic-hypertrophy]]; [[heuristic--isometric-training-execution-protocol-and-advantages-drawbacks]]"}]},"uuid":"9d13413b-9b78-4148-b512-8476676e6cf6","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:16:15.033Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric.md","content":"**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: \"No sport performs true 1RM testing. Maxes are projected from observation plus the athlete's history\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 61, Explicit, Q10), and %1RM prescription is kept \"stated explicitly as built on projected maxes\" (line 260, Tension 16). Inference: the percentage bands here are therefore percentages of a projected max, so the RM descriptions (3-5RM, 8-12 reps) are the safer way to apply them. The baseline's off-season rule (\"relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work,\" line 105, course material, Inferred). Inference: that rule lines up with the authors' sequence, repetitive effort first and brief maximal tension once it stops working. Inference: the in-season rule \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" (line 105) matches the authors' use of brief maximal tension \"for maintaining the existing level of strength.\"\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The book contradicts itself on several details; keep both sides. (1) Sets: brief maximal tension needs sets \"increased to more than three (Berger, 1962)\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260), but its effect \"is enhanced by increasing the maximal weight, increasing the average weight lifted per session and reducing the number of sets and repetitions\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). (2) Size of effect: brief maximal tension \"yields a larger training effect than the progressive resistance method for developing maximum strength\" (Diachkov 1961; Chudinov 1961; PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260), against \"Similar significant increases in maximum strength can be obtained by the repetitive effort and brief maximal tension methods, as well as with isometric tension\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). (3) Isometrics: isometric training \"does not increase strength significantly more\" than dynamic lifting (Salter 1955; PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260) and dynamic 3 x 5-6 \"is more effective than the corresponding amount of isometric work\" (Rasch & Morehouse 1957; Berger 1962; PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261), against the \"similar significant increases ... with isometric tension\" sentence on the same page. (4) Load-rep pairings: the 5RM is \"about 89%\" of 1RM (PDF p. 269 / printed p. 258), yet \"90-95% of 1RM\" is paired with \"3-6\" reps (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261), and \"85-95% of 1RM\" with \"3-5RM\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260). These three are not consistent; do not use any one as a rep-to-load conversion. Inference: for sports decided by fast force production, these three methods are not the whole answer; the authors put speed-strength and explosive work in separate method groups (5.2.3-5.2.4).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own prescription for weightlifters under brief maximal tension: \"5-6 exercises for 6-10 sets of 1-3 repetitions in one training session (Medvedev, 1967; Vorobyev, 1971)\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared]]; [[claim--berger-sets-reps-and-frequency-studies-in-novices]]; [[claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves]]; [[claim--strength-detraining-and-retention-timelines]]; [[framework--types-of-maximal-strength]]; [[framework--sarcomere-vs-sarcoplasmic-hypertrophy]]; [[heuristic--isometric-training-execution-protocol-and-advantages-drawbacks]]","numLines":7,"startLine":23,"totalLines":30}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"bc3b635b-b1af-4d9a-a294-c20af1613613","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
