-10-03\n---\n\n# Berger's Sets, Reps and Frequency Studies: About 3 x 6RM, One Heavy Day a Week Carries the Gain, but the Subjects Were Novices\n\n**What it is** — The authors summarise early Western studies on sets, reps and frequency (PDF p. 270-271 / printed p. 259-260, verified against the page images). Of nine bench-press programmes, \"3 sets of 6 repetitions (with the 6RM weight) was the most effective (Berger, 1962),\" corroborating Keipen (1956) on three sets of the 5RM. Berger (1963) concluded that a heavy weight \"does not permit the optimal number of repetitions,\" and light weights with many reps offer \"too weak a stimulus,\" yet \"A significant difference in strength increase was not found between training regimes using 2RM, 5RM or 10RM,\" and there was \"no major difference between the effectiveness of 2 or 3 training sessions per week.\" \"Training with a rest interval of two days between sessions is significantly more effective than with a rest interval of one day (Vasiliev, 1954).\" Berger (1965) compared five programmes: (1) 2/3 of 1RM twice a week plus the 1RM in the third workout; (2) the same with 80% of 1RM; (3) with 90%; (4) 1RM once a week; (5) 2/3 of 1RM three times a week. \"The only programme which did not produce an increase in strength was the fifth one\"; the others gave \"approximately the same\" gains. The six conclusions include: submaximal twice plus maximal once a week equals maximal three times a week; the gain from programme 1 \"is due mainly to training with the 1RM\"; with three workouts a week the load per set \"should be between the 3RM and the 10RM\"; \"Training once a week with the 1RM for 1 set increases strength significantly for up to 6 weeks\"; twice a week with 3 x 10RM equals three times a week; and after one set of the 10RM, \"subsequent lifts with lighter loads have no training value.\" The authors then stress, in italics, that \"these conclusions were based on research with untrained subjects or subjects with very