mmes, and conclusions 3, 5 and 6 refer to data the page does not describe. Treat those as reported without their data. Berger (1962) favouring 3 x 6RM and Berger (1963) finding no difference between 2RM, 5RM and 10RM regimes are both printed on PDF p. 270 / printed p. 259 without reconciliation. The muscle tested in Berger 1965 and the study lengths are not given.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example is Berger (1965) programme 4, the 1RM once a week, which raised strength about as much as the other programmes except programme 5, and conclusion 4, that one set of the 1RM once a week \"increases strength significantly for up to 6 weeks\" (PDF p. 270-271 / printed p. 259-260).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric]]; [[claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared]]; [[claim--strength-and-speed-training-response-shifts-with-training-age]] (training age changes the effective load); [[heuristic--program-variable-complexity-scales-with-training-age]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":19,"oldLines":7,"newStart":19,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — Eric's baseline: \"No sport performs true 1RM testing\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 61, Explicit, Q10). Inference: the Berger results are about novices, which is the setting where they are most usable: freshmen and athletes new to barbell training gain on modest frequency, and one heavy exposure a week can carry most of the strength gain, which helps when practice load limits lifting days. For advanced lifters the Vorobyev figures (1-6 reps, most volume at 70% or more) are the authors' own guide."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — Do not carry the novice conclusions to trained athletes; the authors say so themselves. Several of the six conclusions are not traceable on the page: conclusion 1 speaks of \"maximal weights three times per week,\" which is none of the five listed programmes, and conclusions 3, 5 and 6 refer to data the page does not describe. Treat those as reported without their data. Berger (1962) favouring 3 x 6RM and Berger (1963) finding no difference between 2RM, 5RM and 10RM regimes are both printed on PDF p. 270 / printed p. 259 without reconciliation. The muscle tested in Berger 1965 and the study lengths are not given.","+**When NOT to apply** — Do not carry the novice conclusions to trained athletes; the authors say so themselves. The six conclusions are drawn partly from data outside the five-programme list: conclusion 1's \"maximal weights three times per week\" and conclusion 5 trace to the Berger (1963) sentences (\"10RM for one set three times per week\"; \"no major difference between the effectiveness of 2 or 3 training sessions per week,\" PDF p. 270 / printed p. 259), though conclusion 5 speaks of \"3 sets of the 10RM\" where that sentence describes one set; conclusion 3 traces loosely to the 2RM/5RM/10RM and 3 x 6RM results; and conclusion 6 traces to no data on the page. Treat conclusion 6 as reported without its data. Berger (1962) favouring 3 x 6RM and Berger (1963) finding no difference between 2RM, 5RM and 10RM regimes are both printed on PDF p. 270 / printed p. 259 without reconciliation. The muscle tested in Berger 1965 and the study lengths are not given."," "," **Example** — The authors' own example is Berger (1965) programme 4, the 1RM once a week, which raised strength about as much as the other programmes except programme 5, and conclusion 4, that one set of the 1RM once a week \"increases strength significantly for up to 6 weeks\" (PDF p. 270-271 / printed p. 259-260)."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false,"contentNotInModelContext":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"325a12f9-5dab-44ec-a5df-c9739bf6f423","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"67d8ccfa-0e77-4771-90f3-31c3b948893d","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ad490fb3266cc50f1","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffbyDaLD32h3vm7pqyQ6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01K5LMPzggxYDebKFcJLbrCA","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves.md","offset":14,"limit":5},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1955,"cache_read_input_tokens":137113,"output_tokens":1144,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":142},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1955},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1144,"cache_read_input_tokens":137113,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1955,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1955,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01K5LMPzggxYDebKFcJLbrCA":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves.md","offset":14,"limit":5}},"apiBlockIndex":3,"requestId":"req_011Cffby6NF8RphxwogxxinH","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"7ec2eb0f-8aa5-41d2-a2fb-1bf1efb4a4ec","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:16:43.186Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"7ec2eb0f-8aa5-41d2-a2fb-1bf1efb4a4ec","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"03e4fd49-e0c7-4b5d-bb77-f25bc8e8f726","agentId":"ad490fb3266cc50f1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01K5LMPzggxYDebKFcJLbrCA","type":"tool_result","content":"14\t# Ivanov (1966): Concentric, Eccentric and Isometric Training Each Improve Their Own Test Most, and Only Concentric Training Raised the Vertical Jump\n15\t\n16\t**What it is** — In \"A 3-month experiment comparing the effectiveness of concentric, eccentric and isometric work,\" \"the largest increase in a 1RM squat occurred with eccentric work (average 15 kg), and the smallest with isometric tension (9.2 kg).\" \"Not unexpectedly, the largest increase in isometric strength was obtained with isometric tension (30.2 kg), and the smallest increase with concentric work (14.6 kg).\" \"Vertical jump increased only with the concentric training regime (3.7 cm)\"; it \"decreased by 1.6 cm for the eccentric regime and by 5.4 cm for isometric tension (Ivanov, 1966)\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261, numbers verified against the page image; the text layer misreads 30.2 as \"iJO.2\"). The authors' conclusion: \"this research does not support the effectiveness of eccentric work as much as it corroborates the principle of the neuromuscular specificity of strength, which dictates the method of its development.\" Around it they report that isometric training does not beat dynamic lifting (Salter 1955; Denison et al 1961; Asmussen 1949; Darcus 1955; Rose et al 1951), that dynamic 3 x 5-6 beats \"the corresponding amount of isometric work (Rasch & Morehouse, 1957; Berger, 1962),\" that \"Comparison with explosive isometrics, however, has not yet been done,\" and that comment on eccentric training \"should be guarded\" until \"more reliable results are obtained\" (PDF p. 271-272 / printed p. 260-261).\n17\t\n18\t**Why it works** — The authors' explanation is \"the neuromuscular specificity of strength\": each mode trains the nervous system for that mode (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). They give no further mechanism."}]},"uuid":"3acd2521-e44e-40c5-810e-f6a4dcc92d17","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:16:43.357Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves.md","content":"# Ivanov (1966): Concentric, Eccentric and Isometric Training Each Improve Their Own Test Most, and Only Concentric Training Raised the Vertical Jump\n\n**What it is** — In \"A 3-month experiment comparing the effectiveness of concentric, eccentric and isometric work,\" \"the largest increase in a 1RM squat occurred with eccentric work (average 15 kg), and the smallest with isometric tension (9.2 kg).\" \"Not unexpectedly, the largest increase in isometric strength was obtained with isometric tension (30.2 kg), and the smallest increase with concentric work (14.6 kg).\" \"Vertical jump increased only with the concentric training regime (3.7 cm)\"; it \"decreased by 1.6 cm for the eccentric regime and by 5.4 cm for isometric tension (Ivanov, 1966)\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261, numbers verified against the page image; the text layer misreads 30.2 as \"iJO.2\"). The authors' conclusion: \"this research does not support the effectiveness of eccentric work as much as it corroborates the principle of the neuromuscular specificity of strength, which dictates the method of its development.\" Around it they report that isometric training does not beat dynamic lifting (Salter 1955; Denison et al 1961; Asmussen 1949; Darcus 1955; Rose et al 1951), that dynamic 3 x 5-6 beats \"the corresponding amount of isometric work (Rasch & Morehouse, 1957; Berger, 1962),\" that \"Comparison with explosive isometrics, however, has not yet been done,\" and that comment on eccentric training \"should be guarded\" until \"more reliable results are obtained\" (PDF p. 271-272 / printed p. 260-261).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' explanation is \"the neuromuscular specificity of strength\": each mode trains the nervous system for that mode (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). They give no further mechanism.","numLines":5,"startLine":14,"totalLines":27}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"7ec2eb0f-8aa5-41d2-a2fb-1bf1efb4a4ec","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
