{"parentUuid":"19676a38-a248-40f0-9e9f-3f680562c9e7","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ad490fb3266cc50f1","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffbz8QogmpTps1XhTPW3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GFLkmHvHsQc14p8cE5Ufu1","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves.md","old_string":"# Ivanov (1966): Concentric, Eccentric and Isometric Training Each Improve Their Own Test Most, and Only Concentric Training Raised the Vertical Jump","new_string":"# Ivanov (1966): Isometric Training Raised Isometric Strength Most, Eccentric Training Raised the Squat 1RM Most, and Only Concentric Training Raised the Vertical Jump"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2089,"cache_read_input_tokens":139068,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2089,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01GFLkmHvHsQc14p8cE5Ufu1":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves.md","old_string":"# Ivanov (1966): Concentric, Eccentric and Isometric Training Each Improve Their Own Test Most, and Only Concentric Training Raised the Vertical Jump","new_string":"# Ivanov (1966): Isometric Training Raised Isometric Strength Most, Eccentric Training Raised the Squat 1RM Most, and Only Concentric Training Raised the Vertical Jump"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cffbyt3N93yttYRRUzhW7","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"466a96ac-fe0e-4f65-9130-b9db483286d5","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:16:49.602Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"466a96ac-fe0e-4f65-9130-b9db483286d5","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"03e4fd49-e0c7-4b5d-bb77-f25bc8e8f726","agentId":"ad490fb3266cc50f1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GFLkmHvHsQc14p8cE5Ufu1","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves.md has been updated successfully."}]},"uuid":"89cd1d2c-5138-4158-9d9b-92fe59ed4f78","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:16:49.714Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves.md","oldString":"# Ivanov (1966): Concentric, Eccentric and Isometric Training Each Improve Their Own Test Most, and Only Concentric Training Raised the Vertical Jump","newString":"# Ivanov (1966): Isometric Training Raised Isometric Strength Most, Eccentric Training Raised the Squat 1RM Most, and Only Concentric Training Raised the Vertical Jump","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.1\"\ntopic: concentric eccentric isometric training comparison\ncategory: [specificity, eccentric, isometric]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Ivanov (1966): Concentric, Eccentric and Isometric Training Each Improve Their Own Test Most, and Only Concentric Training Raised the Vertical Jump\n\n**What it is** — In \"A 3-month experiment comparing the effectiveness of concentric, eccentric and isometric work,\" \"the largest increase in a 1RM squat occurred with eccentric work (average 15 kg), and the smallest with isometric tension (9.2 kg).\" \"Not unexpectedly, the largest increase in isometric strength was obtained with isometric tension (30.2 kg), and the smallest increase with concentric work (14.6 kg).\" \"Vertical jump increased only with the concentric training regime (3.7 cm)\"; it \"decreased by 1.6 cm for the eccentric regime and by 5.4 cm for isometric tension (Ivanov, 1966)\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261, numbers verified against the page image; the text layer misreads 30.2 as \"iJO.2\"). The authors' conclusion: \"this research does not support the effectiveness of eccentric work as much as it corroborates the principle of the neuromuscular specificity of strength, which dictates the method of its development.\" Around it they report that isometric training does not beat dynamic lifting (Salter 1955; Denison et al 1961; Asmussen 1949; Darcus 1955; Rose et al 1951), that dynamic 3 x 5-6 beats \"the corresponding amount of isometric work (Rasch & Morehouse, 1957; Berger, 1962),\" that \"Comparison with explosive isometrics, however, has not yet been done,\" and that comment on eccentric training \"should be guarded\" until \"more reliable results are obtained\" (PDF p. 271-272 / printed p. 260-261).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' explanation is \"the neuromuscular specificity of strength\": each mode trains the nervous system for that mode (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). They give no further mechanism.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: the dynamic strength index from the CMJ and the isometric mid-thigh pull \"chooses between plyometric and max-strength emphasis\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 76, Explicit, Q21; tagged contested there). Inference: Ivanov's result is a reminder that an isometric test (IMTP) will rise most with isometric work and a jump test most with concentric, fast work, so a change in DSI after a block can reflect the training mode as much as a real shift in the athlete's profile. Read the two tests against what was trained.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Group sizes, subjects, exercises and loads for Ivanov (1966) are not given. Inference: a squat 1RM is decided by its concentric phase, yet eccentric training raised it most, which the authors do not discuss; that is a reason not to over-read the specificity story from one study. The \"explosive isometrics\" comparison was not done, so the isometric result applies to slow isometrics only. The book's other eccentric findings disagree in places; see [[claim--eccentric-vs-concentric-training-adaptation-contradictory-findings]].\n\n**Example** — The authors' own data: isometric training gave the largest isometric strength gain (30.2 kg) and lowered the vertical jump by 5.4 cm (Ivanov 1966; PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--velocity-and-contraction-specificity-asymmetric-transfer]] (Ch 1, the same specificity point from other studies); [[claim--eccentric-vs-concentric-training-adaptation-contradictory-findings]]; [[framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric]]; [[heuristic--isometric-training-execution-protocol-and-advantages-drawbacks]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":11,"oldLines":7,"newStart":11,"newLines":7,"lines":[" created: 2026-10-03"," ---"," ","-# Ivanov (1966): Concentric, Eccentric and Isometric Training Each Improve Their Own Test Most, and Only Concentric Training Raised the Vertical Jump","+# Ivanov (1966): Isometric Training Raised Isometric Strength Most, Eccentric Training Raised the Squat 1RM Most, and Only Concentric Training Raised the Vertical Jump"," "," **What it is** — In \"A 3-month experiment comparing the effectiveness of concentric, eccentric and isometric work,\" \"the largest increase in a 1RM squat occurred with eccentric work (average 15 kg), and the smallest with isometric tension (9.2 kg).\" \"Not unexpectedly, the largest increase in isometric strength was obtained with isometric tension (30.2 kg), and the smallest increase with concentric work (14.6 kg).\" \"Vertical jump increased only with the concentric training regime (3.7 cm)\"; it \"decreased by 1.6 cm for the eccentric regime and by 5.4 cm for isometric tension (Ivanov, 1966)\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261, numbers verified against the page image; the text layer misreads 30.2 as \"iJO.2\"). The authors' conclusion: \"this research does not support the effectiveness of eccentric work as much as it corroborates the principle of the neuromuscular specificity of strength, which dictates the method of its development.\" Around it they report that isometric training does not beat dynamic lifting (Salter 1955; Denison et al 1961; Asmussen 1949; Darcus 1955; Rose et al 1951), that dynamic 3 x 5-6 beats \"the corresponding amount of isometric work (Rasch & Morehouse, 1957; Berger, 1962),\" that \"Comparison with explosive isometrics, however, has not yet been done,\" and that comment on eccentric training \"should be guarded\" until \"more reliable results are obtained\" (PDF p. 271-272 / printed p. 260-261)."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false,"contentNotInModelContext":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"466a96ac-fe0e-4f65-9130-b9db483286d5","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"89cd1d2c-5138-4158-9d9b-92fe59ed4f78","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ad490fb3266cc50f1","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffbz8QogmpTps1XhTPW3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HzMYmna51CHNTJtfJco2Zb","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves.md","old_string":"\"the neuromuscular specificity of strength\": each mode trains the nervous system for that mode (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). They give no further mechanism.","new_string":"\"the neuromuscular specificity of strength\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). They give no further mechanism. Inference: read as each mode training the nervous system for that mode; the book does not say this."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2089,"cache_read_input_tokens":139068,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2089,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01HzMYmna51CHNTJtfJco2Zb":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves.md","old_string":"\"the neuromuscular specificity of strength\": each mode trains the nervous system for that mode (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). They give no further mechanism.","new_string":"\"the neuromuscular specificity of strength\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). They give no further mechanism. Inference: read as each mode training the nervous system for that mode; the book does not say this."}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffbyt3N93yttYRRUzhW7","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"fa31d362-c025-4e79-8553-19a07f09fdc3","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:16:51.371Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"fa31d362-c025-4e79-8553-19a07f09fdc3","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"03e4fd49-e0c7-4b5d-bb77-f25bc8e8f726","agentId":"ad490fb3266cc50f1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HzMYmna51CHNTJtfJco2Zb","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves.md has been updated successfully."}]},"uuid":"df541289-b0f2-4369-954a-5e9c3de9c09d","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:16:51.461Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves.md","oldString":"\"the neuromuscular specificity of strength\": each mode trains the nervous system for that mode (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). They give no further mechanism.","newString":"\"the neuromuscular specificity of strength\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). They give no further mechanism. Inference: read as each mode training the nervous system for that mode; the book does not say this.","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.1\"\ntopic: concentric eccentric isometric training comparison\ncategory: [specificity, eccentric, isometric]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Ivanov (1966): Isometric Training Raised Isometric Strength Most, Eccentric Training Raised the Squat 1RM Most, and Only Concentric Training Raised the Vertical Jump\n\n**What it is** — In \"A 3-month experiment comparing the effectiveness of concentric, eccentric and isometric work,\" \"the largest increase in a 1RM squat occurred with eccentric work (average 15 kg), and the smallest with isometric tension (9.2 kg).\" \"Not unexpectedly, the largest increase in isometric strength was obtained with isometric tension (30.2 kg), and the smallest increase with concentric work (14.6 kg).\" \"Vertical jump increased only with the concentric training regime (3.7 cm)\"; it \"decreased by 1.6 cm for the eccentric regime and by 5.4 cm for isometric tension (Ivanov, 1966)\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261, numbers verified against the page image; the text layer misreads 30.2 as \"iJO.2\"). The authors' conclusion: \"this research does not support the effectiveness of eccentric work as much as it corroborates the principle of the neuromuscular specificity of strength, which dictates the method of its development.\" Around it they report that isometric training does not beat dynamic lifting (Salter 1955; Denison et al 1961; Asmussen 1949; Darcus 1955; Rose et al 1951), that dynamic 3 x 5-6 beats \"the corresponding amount of isometric work (Rasch & Morehouse, 1957; Berger, 1962),\" that \"Comparison with explosive isometrics, however, has not yet been done,\" and that comment on eccentric training \"should be guarded\" until \"more reliable results are obtained\" (PDF p. 271-272 / printed p. 260-261).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' explanation is \"the neuromuscular specificity of strength\": each mode trains the nervous system for that mode (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). They give no further mechanism.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: the dynamic strength index from the CMJ and the isometric mid-thigh pull \"chooses between plyometric and max-strength emphasis\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 76, Explicit, Q21; tagged contested there). Inference: Ivanov's result is a reminder that an isometric test (IMTP) will rise most with isometric work and a jump test most with concentric, fast work, so a change in DSI after a block can reflect the training mode as much as a real shift in the athlete's profile. Read the two tests against what was trained.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Group sizes, subjects, exercises and loads for Ivanov (1966) are not given. Inference: a squat 1RM is decided by its concentric phase, yet eccentric training raised it most, which the authors do not discuss; that is a reason not to over-read the specificity story from one study. The \"explosive isometrics\" comparison was not done, so the isometric result applies to slow isometrics only. The book's other eccentric findings disagree in places; see [[claim--eccentric-vs-concentric-training-adaptation-contradictory-findings]].\n\n**Example** — The authors' own data: isometric training gave the largest isometric strength gain (30.2 kg) and lowered the vertical jump by 5.4 cm (Ivanov 1966; PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--velocity-and-contraction-specificity-asymmetric-transfer]] (Ch 1, the same specificity point from other studies); [[claim--eccentric-vs-concentric-training-adaptation-contradictory-findings]]; [[framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric]]; [[heuristic--isometric-training-execution-protocol-and-advantages-drawbacks]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":15,"oldLines":7,"newStart":15,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **What it is** — In \"A 3-month experiment comparing the effectiveness of concentric, eccentric and isometric work,\" \"the largest increase in a 1RM squat occurred with eccentric work (average 15 kg), and the smallest with isometric tension (9.2 kg).\" \"Not unexpectedly, the largest increase in isometric strength was obtained with isometric tension (30.2 kg), and the smallest increase with concentric work (14.6 kg).\" \"Vertical jump increased only with the concentric training regime (3.7 cm)\"; it \"decreased by 1.6 cm for the eccentric regime and by 5.4 cm for isometric tension (Ivanov, 1966)\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261, numbers verified against the page image; the text layer misreads 30.2 as \"iJO.2\"). The authors' conclusion: \"this research does not support the effectiveness of eccentric work as much as it corroborates the principle of the neuromuscular specificity of strength, which dictates the method of its development.\" Around it they report that isometric training does not beat dynamic lifting (Salter 1955; Denison et al 1961; Asmussen 1949; Darcus 1955; Rose et al 1951), that dynamic 3 x 5-6 beats \"the corresponding amount of isometric work (Rasch & Morehouse, 1957; Berger, 1962),\" that \"Comparison with explosive isometrics, however, has not yet been done,\" and that comment on eccentric training \"should be guarded\" until \"more reliable results are obtained\" (PDF p. 271-272 / printed p. 260-261)."," ","-**Why it works** — The authors' explanation is \"the neuromuscular specificity of strength\": each mode trains the nervous system for that mode (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). They give no further mechanism.","+**Why it works** — The authors' explanation is \"the neuromuscular specificity of strength\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). They give no further mechanism. Inference: read as each mode training the nervous system for that mode; the book does not say this."," "," **When to apply** — Eric's baseline: the dynamic strength index from the CMJ and the isometric mid-thigh pull \"chooses between plyometric and max-strength emphasis\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 76, Explicit, Q21; tagged contested there). Inference: Ivanov's result is a reminder that an isometric test (IMTP) will rise most with isometric work and a jump test most with concentric, fast work, so a change in DSI after a block can reflect the training mode as much as a real shift in the athlete's profile. Read the two tests against what was trained."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false,"contentNotInModelContext":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"fa31d362-c025-4e79-8553-19a07f09fdc3","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"df541289-b0f2-4369-954a-5e9c3de9c09d","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ad490fb3266cc50f1","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffbz8QogmpTps1XhTPW3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014KAcWKZbPyZ23zB5pM118t","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-stay-close-to-competition-mass.md","offset":14,"limit":13},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2089,"cache_read_input_tokens":139068,"output_tokens":752,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2089},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":752,"cache_read_input_tokens":139068,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2089,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2089,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_014KAcWKZbPyZ23zB5pM118t":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-stay-close-to-competition-mass.md","offset":14,"limit":13}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011Cffbyt3N93yttYRRUzhW7","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"f171a16d-63ae-4d0c-8d88-9395bf3d58fb","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:16:52.437Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"f171a16d-63ae-4d0c-8d88-9395bf3d58fb","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"03e4fd49-e0c7-4b5d-bb77-f25bc8e8f726","agentId":"ad490fb3266cc50f1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014KAcWKZbPyZ23zB5pM118t","type":"tool_result","content":"14\t# Lighter and Heavier Implements for Speed-Strength: Small Deviations From the Competition Mass Work Better, and Transfer Can Run One Way\n15\t\n16\t**What it is** — For acyclic speed-strength the authors say \"plyometric or throwing exercises should be selected on the basis of their relevance to the sports activity,\" and give a run of implement findings (PDF p. 276-277 / printed p. 265-266, verified against the page images). Water polo throw: a 2 kg medicine ball beat a 4 kg ball, throwing distance up \"13.6% and 8.9%, respectively,\" and \"throwing a 4 kg ball is detrimental to technique (Rogener, 1961).\" Baseball: a light \"57 grams\" and heavy \"184 gm\" ball both improved throwing (regulation \"about 148 gm\"), but \"throwing the light ball improves precision for throwing the heavier ball, but the reverse effect does not occur (Egstrom et al, 1960).\" Javelin: \"The optimal weight for javelin training which does not disrupt technique is 3 kg (Matveyev, 1967).\" Shot: alternating shots \"differing in mass by 250 grams\" gave distances \"close to the mean,\" but \"this closeness to the average was not achieved with a difference of 500 grams (Ivanova, 1964; Vasiliev, 1975),\" so \"the transference of throwing speeds from a lighter to a heavier mass ... apparently is possible only by alternating the throwing of shots of different mass.\" Ice hockey: hitting a normal then a heavier puck confirmed the variable method; the heavier puck's optimal mass \"was found to be between 0.6-0.8 kg,\" selected \"for each player individually, based on the athlete's ability to hit the heavier puck efficiently (Savin, 1974).\" Table tennis: some Eastern European and Chinese players train with bats up to \"2kg,\" against typical bats of \"about 100 grams,\" only \"carefully balanced,\" by \"elite athletes about once a week before the competition phase\"; the authors warn this \"should not be emulated by less experienced coaches,\" because untutored use \"can lead to overload injuries and altered neural control patterns.\"\n17\t\n18\t**Why it works** — The authors' reason is that too heavy an implement disrupts technique; they give no further mechanism. Inference: the Egstrom result (light-to-heavy transfer of precision, not the reverse) is consistent with the book's Ch 1 finding that high-velocity training transfers to low velocity better than the reverse, but the authors do not draw that link here.\n19\t\n20\t**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: Dynamic Correspondence \"governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 103, Explicit, Q1). Inference: for throwing and striking positions (quarterbacks, baseball and softball), these findings argue for overweight and underweight implements kept close to the competition mass, alternated with the normal implement, with the heavy option sized per athlete by whether technique holds. Bondarchuk's correlation data point the same way: \"The greatest amounts of correlational interrelationships were most frequently found in cases where the deviations between the lighter and heavier implement weights was insignificant\" ([[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/heuristic--implement-weight-dosing-and-throwing-boom-warning]]).\n21\t\n22\t**When NOT to apply** — The shot finding is muddy as printed: \"A statistically significant difference was not found in the mean results,\" and the 250 g advantage is described only as distances \"close to the mean,\" yet the authors conclude transfer is \"possible only\" by alternating masses. Treat that conclusion as stronger than the data shown. The baseball result concerns precision, not velocity. No group sizes or training durations are given for any study. The 2 kg table-tennis bat is an anecdote from coaches, with the authors' own warning against copying it.\n23\t\n24\t**Example** — The authors' own example: water polo players gained more throwing distance with a 2 kg medicine ball (13.6%) than with a 4 kg ball (8.9%), and the 4 kg ball harmed technique (Rogener 1961; PDF p. 276 / printed p. 265).\n25\t\n26\t**Related** — [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/heuristic--implement-weight-dosing-and-throwing-boom-warning]] (agrees: close to competition weight transfers best); [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/claim--throws-implement-weight-transfer-near-universal]]; [[claim--velocity-and-contraction-specificity-asymmetric-transfer]]; [[heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix]]; [[case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work]]"}]},"uuid":"a8be8fed-6e8c-48a6-a804-dec6d0e8eed8","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:16:52.594Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-stay-close-to-competition-mass.md","content":"# Lighter and Heavier Implements for Speed-Strength: Small Deviations From the Competition Mass Work Better, and Transfer Can Run One Way\n\n**What it is** — For acyclic speed-strength the authors say \"plyometric or throwing exercises should be selected on the basis of their relevance to the sports activity,\" and give a run of implement findings (PDF p. 276-277 / printed p. 265-266, verified against the page images). Water polo throw: a 2 kg medicine ball beat a 4 kg ball, throwing distance up \"13.6% and 8.9%, respectively,\" and \"throwing a 4 kg ball is detrimental to technique (Rogener, 1961).\" Baseball: a light \"57 grams\" and heavy \"184 gm\" ball both improved throwing (regulation \"about 148 gm\"), but \"throwing the light ball improves precision for throwing the heavier ball, but the reverse effect does not occur (Egstrom et al, 1960).\" Javelin: \"The optimal weight for javelin training which does not disrupt technique is 3 kg (Matveyev, 1967).\" Shot: alternating shots \"differing in mass by 250 grams\" gave distances \"close to the mean,\" but \"this closeness to the average was not achieved with a difference of 500 grams (Ivanova, 1964; Vasiliev, 1975),\" so \"the transference of throwing speeds from a lighter to a heavier mass ... apparently is possible only by alternating the throwing of shots of different mass.\" Ice hockey: hitting a normal then a heavier puck confirmed the variable method; the heavier puck's optimal mass \"was found to be between 0.6-0.8 kg,\" selected \"for each player individually, based on the athlete's ability to hit the heavier puck efficiently (Savin, 1974).\" Table tennis: some Eastern European and Chinese players train with bats up to \"2kg,\" against typical bats of \"about 100 grams,\" only \"carefully balanced,\" by \"elite athletes about once a week before the competition phase\"; the authors warn this \"should not be emulated by less experienced coaches,\" because untutored use \"can lead to overload injuries and altered neural control patterns.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is that too heavy an implement disrupts technique; they give no further mechanism. Inference: the Egstrom result (light-to-heavy transfer of precision, not the reverse) is consistent with the book's Ch 1 finding that high-velocity training transfers to low velocity better than the reverse, but the authors do not draw that link here.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: Dynamic Correspondence \"governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 103, Explicit, Q1). Inference: for throwing and striking positions (quarterbacks, baseball and softball), these findings argue for overweight and underweight implements kept close to the competition mass, alternated with the normal implement, with the heavy option sized per athlete by whether technique holds. Bondarchuk's correlation data point the same way: \"The greatest amounts of correlational interrelationships were most frequently found in cases where the deviations between the lighter and heavier implement weights was insignificant\" ([[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/heuristic--implement-weight-dosing-and-throwing-boom-warning]]).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The shot finding is muddy as printed: \"A statistically significant difference was not found in the mean results,\" and the 250 g advantage is described only as distances \"close to the mean,\" yet the authors conclude transfer is \"possible only\" by alternating masses. Treat that conclusion as stronger than the data shown. The baseball result concerns precision, not velocity. No group sizes or training durations are given for any study. The 2 kg table-tennis bat is an anecdote from coaches, with the authors' own warning against copying it.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example: water polo players gained more throwing distance with a 2 kg medicine ball (13.6%) than with a 4 kg ball (8.9%), and the 4 kg ball harmed technique (Rogener 1961; PDF p. 276 / printed p. 265).\n\n**Related** — [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/heuristic--implement-weight-dosing-and-throwing-boom-warning]] (agrees: close to competition weight transfers best); [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/claim--throws-implement-weight-transfer-near-universal]]; [[claim--velocity-and-contraction-specificity-asymmetric-transfer]]; [[heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix]]; [[case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work]]","numLines":13,"startLine":14,"totalLines":27}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f171a16d-63ae-4d0c-8d88-9395bf3d58fb","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
