{"parentUuid":"ee976c51-8283-4575-8dca-45e86079f7d7","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"03e4fd49-e0c7-4b5d-bb77-f25bc8e8f726","agentId":"ad490fb3266cc50f1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013qDvmYAPrQL8gz5CfKGwoc","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix.md has been updated successfully."}]},"uuid":"af0c38e6-5a04-4420-887f-67abd9518f83","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:17:18.213Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix.md","oldString":"[[claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-stay-close-to-competition-mass]]","newString":"[[claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete]]","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.3\"\ntopic: speed-strength loading\ncategory: [speed-strength, loading, programming]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Speed-Strength Loading: Mostly About 20% of 1RM at Maximal Acceleration, Mixed 5:1 With Loads up to 40%, Plus Fast Isometrics at 60-80%\n\n**What it is** — \"The development of speed-strength is more effective the more speed loading there is in training and the shorter the time spent using slow movements (Yakovlev et al, 1960).\" \"Exercise with small loads (with approximately 20% of 1RM) is the principal method of developing speed-strength\" (Korobkov 1953; Vasiliev 1954; Gerasimov, Yakhontov 1954; Zimkin 1956; Agdgomelashvili 1964; Butenko 1967) (PDF p. 275-276 / printed p. 264-265, verified against the page images). With it, \"speed of movement increases with and without loads, and experiments have shown that the general increase in speed can reach 146% of the initial level.\" \"Movements should be executed with maximal effort and the apparatus should be accelerated as rapidly as possible.\" To promote recruitment, light and heavier weights \"(up to 40% of 1RM) should be combined (the variable method)\" with \"emphasis on acceleration at the beginning of the movement,\" plus plyometrics and \"exercises where isometric tension is generated quickly using 60-80% of 1RM.\" \"The optimal combination of the volume of exercises with light weights and relatively heavy weights should be in a ratio of 5:1,\" and \"the alternation of exercises is most effective for the ranked athlete\" (PDF p. 276 / printed p. 265). The section summary repeats it: \"exercises, primarily with small resistance (20% of 1RM) and, for acyclic activities of brief duration, a combination of these exercises with weights up to 40% of 1RM in a ratio of 5 to 1,\" with the regime matching the special exercise (cyclic or acyclic) and its starting condition (\"muscles relaxed, in a state of preliminary tension or stretched\") (PDF p. 278 / printed p. 267). The load-versus-speed conflict may be resolved if strength opposes \"not the weight (gravitational force), but its inertia,\" a method \"very seldom used in practice\" (PDF p. 277 / printed p. 266). The section closes on two levers: \"Perfecting the methods of developing speed-strength is a result of the search for a specific combination of means that exploit\" \"the positive after-effect of preceding work on the subsequent activity\" and \"the use of exercises in which muscular force opposes the inertia of the load and not its weight\" (PDF p. 278 / printed p. 267, verified against the page image). The after-effect lever is the arm-throw experiment in [[case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work]].\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons are training specificity (more fast loading, less slow work) and recruitment \"in the active state\" from mixing light and heavier loads. They give no physiological mechanism beyond that.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline (course material, Inferred): \"already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work\" in the off-season (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 105), and training time is \"weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints)\" (line 104). Inference: the 20% load, maximal acceleration and the 5:1 light-to-heavier ratio give a concrete dose for that speed-strength block, for example jump squats or throws with light loads making up most of the volume and loads up to 40% the rest.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — \"146% of the initial level\" is ambiguous (to 146% or by 146%), the speed measure is not named, and the cited studies are 1953-1967; quote it as written, not as an effect size. The \"5:1\" ratio has no cited source on the page. \"Alternation ... is most effective for the ranked athlete\" gives no data or definition of \"ranked.\" Percentages of 1RM rest on a 1RM the department does not test (line 61, Explicit, Q10), so the 20% and 40% loads are percentages of a projected max. The inertia method is atomized in [[framework--two-force-regimes-overcoming-weight-vs-inertia]]; the authors themselves say they cannot give \"specific recommendations for its application.\"\n\n**Example** — The authors' own summary prescription for brief acyclic actions: mainly 20% of 1RM, combined with loads up to 40% of 1RM in a ratio of 5 to 1 (PDF p. 278 / printed p. 267).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--two-groups-of-speed-strength-movements-and-their-tension-types]]; [[framework--two-force-regimes-overcoming-weight-vs-inertia]]; [[claim--power-output-peaks-then-declines-with-load-jump-vs-leg-press]]; [[framework--compensatory-acceleration-training]]; [[claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-stay-close-to-competition-mass]]; [[heuristic--cyclic-vs-acyclic-speed-strength-tempo-rest-and-fatigue]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":23,"oldLines":4,"newStart":23,"newLines":4,"lines":[" "," **Example** — The authors' own summary prescription for brief acyclic actions: mainly 20% of 1RM, combined with loads up to 40% of 1RM in a ratio of 5 to 1 (PDF p. 278 / printed p. 267)."," ","-**Related** — [[framework--two-groups-of-speed-strength-movements-and-their-tension-types]]; [[framework--two-force-regimes-overcoming-weight-vs-inertia]]; [[claim--power-output-peaks-then-declines-with-load-jump-vs-leg-press]]; [[framework--compensatory-acceleration-training]]; [[claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-stay-close-to-competition-mass]]; [[heuristic--cyclic-vs-acyclic-speed-strength-tempo-rest-and-fatigue]]","+**Related** — [[framework--two-groups-of-speed-strength-movements-and-their-tension-types]]; [[framework--two-force-regimes-overcoming-weight-vs-inertia]]; [[claim--power-output-peaks-then-declines-with-load-jump-vs-leg-press]]; [[framework--compensatory-acceleration-training]]; [[claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete]]; [[heuristic--cyclic-vs-acyclic-speed-strength-tempo-rest-and-fatigue]]"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false,"contentNotInModelContext":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ee976c51-8283-4575-8dca-45e86079f7d7","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"af0c38e6-5a04-4420-887f-67abd9518f83","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ad490fb3266cc50f1","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffc1zQQB9pZHsrZYm77T","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017LoGkKjSELWs4cv5HLEQB8","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric.md","old_string":"[[claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves]]","new_string":"[[claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves]]"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1766,"cache_read_input_tokens":145785,"output_tokens":990,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":93},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1766},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":990,"cache_read_input_tokens":145785,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1766,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1766,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_017LoGkKjSELWs4cv5HLEQB8":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric.md","old_string":"[[claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves]]","new_string":"[[claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves]]"}},"apiBlockIndex":3,"requestId":"req_011Cffc1sXuHZw7GiCFM2GZM","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"db50436a-3f29-4f3a-8d0b-627c2e4124ce","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:17:19.513Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"db50436a-3f29-4f3a-8d0b-627c2e4124ce","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"03e4fd49-e0c7-4b5d-bb77-f25bc8e8f726","agentId":"ad490fb3266cc50f1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017LoGkKjSELWs4cv5HLEQB8","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric.md has been updated successfully."}]},"uuid":"948466bb-bd5f-4e3c-b468-b3bb13219155","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:17:19.619Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric.md","oldString":"[[claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves]]","newString":"[[claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves]]","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.1\"\ntopic: maximum strength methods\ncategory: [maximal-strength, programming, hypertrophy]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Three Methods for Maximum Strength (Repetitive Effort, Brief Maximal Tension, Isometric) and When Each Is Used\n\n**What it is** — Maximum strength in this chapter is \"the maximal force that a person can develop voluntarily,\" shown \"under isometric conditions or in a very slow movement against a maximal load.\" There are \"two basic methods\": \"the method of repetitive effort and the method of brief maximal tension\" (PDF p. 269 / printed p. 258), with isometrics discussed as a third option (PDF p. 271-272 / printed p. 260-261). The authors' summary (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261, verified against the page image):\n- **Repetitive effort**: lifting a weight that is raised as strength grows; \"appropriate in the initial stages of strength development and when strength and its speed of production are not important.\" Moderate weights \"(50-60% of 1RM)\" for \"8-12\" reps increase muscle mass; heavy weights \"(90-95% of 1RM)\" for \"3-6\" reps raise strength faster with a smaller mass gain. The effect grows with \"the magnitude of the load and the volume of work.\" The authors attribute its effect to proprioceptive feedback from slow lifting, strong cortex-to-muscle excitation and more motor units recruited (Kabat 1947; Hellebrant & Houtz 1956; PDF p. 269 / printed p. 258).\n- **Brief maximal tension**: \"heavy weights (85-95% of 1RM, i.e. 3-5RM)\" combined with lighter weights in the session and maximal singles \"once or twice a week\"; weightlifters \"execute 5-6 exercises for 6-10 sets of 1-3 repetitions in one training session (Medvedev, 1967; Vorobyev, 1971)\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260). It raises maximum strength \"without appreciably increasing muscle mass,\" improves \"the ability to display strength quickly,\" suits athletes needing relative strength, and is used \"when the repetitive effort method has become ineffective,\" when strength must rise \"quickly in a relatively short period with a small volume of work,\" and to maintain strength and \"sporting form\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261).\n- **Isometric tension (with gradual increase in effort)**: maximum strength without mass gain; to maintain strength \"where speed of movement is not important,\" in preparatory training, and \"during the early stages of musculoskeletal rehabilitation, when movement might be restricted.\" Isometric tension \"may be applied slowly or explosively (see 4.2.4)\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' explanation is neuromuscular specificity: \"Similar significant increases in maximum strength can be obtained\" by all three, \"However, the strength acquired from each of these methods has its own neuromuscular specificity\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). For brief maximal tension they say it \"develops the ability to concentrate neuromuscular effort\" and that lifting near-maximal weights \"enhances the ability to accelerate heavy loads\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260). They also note that strength \"is retained for longer if it is founded first upon a base of increased muscle hypertrophy\" and declines faster if built mainly on neuromuscular efficiency (PDF p. 269 / printed p. 258); that retention point is atomized in [[claim--strength-detraining-and-retention-timelines]].\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: \"No sport performs true 1RM testing. Maxes are projected from observation plus the athlete's history\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 61, Explicit, Q10), and %1RM prescription is kept \"stated explicitly as built on projected maxes\" (line 260, Tension 16). Inference: the percentage bands here are therefore percentages of a projected max, so the RM descriptions (3-5RM, 8-12 reps) are the safer way to apply them. Eric's baseline: off-season strength work prioritizes \"relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work\" (line 105, Inferred, transcript and shared slides). Inference: that rule lines up with the authors' sequence, repetitive effort first and brief maximal tension once it stops working. Inference: the in-season rule \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" (line 105) matches the authors' use of brief maximal tension \"for maintaining the existing level of strength.\"\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The book contradicts itself on several details; keep both sides. (1) Sets: brief maximal tension needs sets \"increased to more than three (Berger, 1962)\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260), but its effect \"is enhanced by increasing the maximal weight, increasing the average weight lifted per session and reducing the number of sets and repetitions\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). (2) Size of effect: brief maximal tension \"yields a larger training effect than the progressive resistance method for developing maximum strength\" (Diachkov 1961; Chudinov 1961; PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260), against \"Similar significant increases in maximum strength can be obtained by the repetitive effort and brief maximal tension methods, as well as with isometric tension\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). (3) Isometrics: isometric training \"does not increase strength significantly more\" than dynamic lifting (Salter 1955; PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260) and dynamic 3 x 5-6 \"is more effective than the corresponding amount of isometric work\" (Rasch & Morehouse 1957; Berger 1962; PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261), against the \"similar significant increases ... with isometric tension\" sentence on the same page. (4) Load-rep pairings: the 5RM is \"about 89%\" of 1RM (PDF p. 269 / printed p. 258), yet \"90-95% of 1RM\" is paired with \"3-6\" reps (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261), and \"85-95% of 1RM\" with \"3-5RM\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260). Inference: the 90-95% with 3-6 reps pairing clearly clashes with 5RM = 89% (six reps cannot be done at 90% or more if five reps is 89%); the 85-95% = 3-5RM pairing is only loosely off (it implies a 5RM near 85%). Do not use any of them as a rep-to-load conversion. Inference: for sports decided by fast force production, these three methods are not the whole answer; the authors put speed-strength and explosive work in separate method groups (5.2.3-5.2.4).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own prescription for weightlifters under brief maximal tension: \"5-6 exercises for 6-10 sets of 1-3 repetitions in one training session (Medvedev, 1967; Vorobyev, 1971)\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared]]; [[claim--berger-sets-reps-and-frequency-studies-in-novices]]; [[claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves]]; [[claim--strength-detraining-and-retention-timelines]]; [[framework--types-of-maximal-strength]]; [[framework--sarcomere-vs-sarcoplasmic-hypertrophy]]; [[heuristic--isometric-training-execution-protocol-and-advantages-drawbacks]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":26,"oldLines":4,"newStart":26,"newLines":4,"lines":[" "," **Example** — The authors' own prescription for weightlifters under brief maximal tension: \"5-6 exercises for 6-10 sets of 1-3 repetitions in one training session (Medvedev, 1967; Vorobyev, 1971)\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260)."," ","-**Related** — [[claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared]]; [[claim--berger-sets-reps-and-frequency-studies-in-novices]]; [[claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves]]; [[claim--strength-detraining-and-retention-timelines]]; [[framework--types-of-maximal-strength]]; [[framework--sarcomere-vs-sarcoplasmic-hypertrophy]]; [[heuristic--isometric-training-execution-protocol-and-advantages-drawbacks]]","+**Related** — [[claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared]]; [[claim--berger-sets-reps-and-frequency-studies-in-novices]]; [[claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves]]; [[claim--strength-detraining-and-retention-timelines]]; [[framework--types-of-maximal-strength]]; [[framework--sarcomere-vs-sarcoplasmic-hypertrophy]]; [[heuristic--isometric-training-execution-protocol-and-advantages-drawbacks]]"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false,"contentNotInModelContext":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"db50436a-3f29-4f3a-8d0b-627c2e4124ce","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
