{"parentUuid":"19ef5940-28d1-41cb-800a-df8d160bbc4f","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ad490fb3266cc50f1","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffc5AyjBfi9Fnbm1ibw2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lint is clean apart from the known `_book.md` false positive. Writing the log."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":239,"cache_read_input_tokens":153890,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":239,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffc53Lqg6H3Mgx8ebY8G","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"f37b67cf-4d92-447c-8c85-2a7a0974cd97","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:17:57.114Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"f37b67cf-4d92-447c-8c85-2a7a0974cd97","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ad490fb3266cc50f1","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffc5AyjBfi9Fnbm1ibw2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013oJc73ttWUwXXN7CYdwXNx","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch5a_applied.md","content":"# Fixes applied: Supertraining 5.1-5.2.3 (Ch5a), 19 entries\n\nFixer: Claude Opus. Date: 2026-10-03. Review: `staging/review_supertraining_ch5a_opus.md` (round 1, GO AFTER FIXES). The review's header counts \"FIX 8\", but its table has 7 FIX rows. Item 8 of its fix list is the inbound-link update after the two renames, not an eighth entry. All 7 rows, item 8, and the two \"Missing\" gaps were handled.\nSource checked: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`. I extracted the text of PDF idx 266-278 with PyMuPDF and rendered them to `staging/_renders/f5a_p<N>.png`. I opened the images for PDF 267 (Newton II, ΔV definition, Fig 5.1, neuromuscular paragraph), 268 (dynamic correspondence), 269 (5RM 89%, DeLorme), 270 (variants, Berger, 1-3 reps sentence), 271 (Berger conclusions 4-6, 85-95% = 3-5RM), 272 (Ivanov, 90-95% / 3-6), 276-277 (implements, Table 5.3) and 278 (two-lever summary). Page key: PDF idx = printed + 11.\nScope: the 19 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch5a.txt`. Only the 7 flagged entries were edited, plus the speed-strength loading entry (inbound link and gap 2). `_book.md` was not touched. No vault file was edited.\n\n| file | what changed |\n|---|---|\n| claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve.md | **What it is:** the `proven` scope sentence now reads \"(∫F_net dt = mΔv, with Δv the change in velocity, not the book's ΔV)\". Scope unchanged: the identity only.<br>**Why it works:** \"the take-off velocity of a jump fixes the net impulse\" is now \"for a jump from a still start, the take-off velocity fixes the net impulse\".<br>**When to apply:** \"propulsive impulse\" is now \"net propulsive impulse (vertical ground reaction force minus body weight, integrated over the propulsive phase, which starts at zero vertical velocity at the bottom of the countermovement)\".<br>**When NOT:** \"Four sentences\" is now \"Five sentences\". In (c), \"net force on a free body\" is now \"net external force on the body\". In (d), the circular-motion example is replaced: \"any change in the velocity vector, direction alone included, requires a net force, but the force itself need not change. A projectile under gravity (ignoring air resistance) changes direction continuously while the force on it stays constant, so a change in velocity direction does not require a change in force, only a net force.\" New (e) quotes the book's ΔV definition, \"The final velocity reached at the end of this phase\", and the 0-to-ΔV integral (PDF 267 / p.256, image). Then: \"Inference: so m.ΔV equals the change in momentum only when the body starts from rest; in general the impulse of the net external force equals m(v_final - v_initial).\" The closing line is now \"Do not use (c), (d) or (e) as stated.\" `confidence: proven` kept. |\n| heuristic--dynamic-correspondence-means-matching-motor-characteristics-not-copying-the-sport-movement.md | **What it is:** \"The paragraph before 5.1's dynamics discussion\" is now \"The paragraph that closes 5.1's dynamics discussion, after Fig 5.1\". The image confirms it: the neuromuscular paragraph follows Fig 5.1 at the foot of PDF 267, and \"All subsequent training ...\" opens PDF 268.<br>**When to apply:** \"This passage is the authors' own statement ...\" is now \"Inference: this passage is the authors' own statement ...\". |\n| framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric.md | **When NOT (4):** \"These three are not consistent; do not use any one as a rep-to-load conversion.\" is replaced. New text: \"Inference: the 90-95% with 3-6 reps pairing clearly clashes with 5RM = 89% (six reps cannot be done at 90% or more if five reps is 89%); the 85-95% = 3-5RM pairing is only loosely off (it implies a 5RM near 85%). Do not use any of them as a rep-to-load conversion.\"<br>**When to apply:** the sentence fragment is rewritten: \"Eric's baseline: off-season strength work prioritizes \"relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work\" (line 105, Inferred, transcript and shared slides).\" The baseline's own tag is \"Inferred (transcript + shared slides)\".<br>**Related:** the Ivanov link now points to the new slug. |\n| claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared.md | **When NOT:** \"All the studies are 1945-1960 work, mostly in rehabilitation or untrained subjects.\" is now \"All the studies are 1945-1960 work; the book gives no subject populations for them. Inference: the DeLorme lineage began in rehabilitation, so untrained or patient samples are likely, but the page does not say so.\" |\n| claim--berger-sets-reps-and-frequency-studies-in-novices.md | **When NOT:** the traceability sentence is replaced. New text: \"The six conclusions are drawn partly from data outside the five-programme list: conclusion 1's \"maximal weights three times per week\" and conclusion 5 trace to the Berger (1963) sentences (...PDF p. 270 / printed p. 259), though conclusion 5 speaks of \"3 sets of the 10RM\" where that sentence describes one set; conclusion 3 traces loosely to the 2RM/5RM/10RM and 3 x 6RM results; and conclusion 6 traces to no data on the page. Treat conclusion 6 as reported without its data.\"<br>**What it is (gap 1):** added after the Vorobyev (1971) clause: \"and \"Other studies\" show that \"most weightlifting training is done with 1-3 repetitions for advanced weightlifters, thereby emphasizing the importance of 1RM, 2RM and 3RM loads for enhancing competitive strength, especially where strength-skill is involved\" (PDF p. 270 / printed p. 259, verified against the page image; Vorobyev 1978 at PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260)\". The old combined locator \"PDF p. 270-271\" was split so each quote has its page. |\n| claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves.md (was `claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves.md`) | **Renamed** with `mv`.<br>**Title** is now \"Ivanov (1966): Isometric Training Raised Isometric Strength Most, Eccentric Training Raised the Squat 1RM Most, and Only Concentric Training Raised the Vertical Jump\". Checked against PDF 272 / p.261 (image): squat 1RM eccentric 15 kg (largest), isometric strength isometric 30.2 kg (largest), vertical jump +3.7 cm with concentric training only. There is no eccentric test.<br>**Why it works:** the gloss is moved out of the authors' voice. New text: \"\"the neuromuscular specificity of strength\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). They give no further mechanism. Inference: read as each mode training the nervous system for that mode; the book does not say this.\" |\n| claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete.md (was `claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-stay-close-to-competition-mass.md`) | **Renamed** with `mv`.<br>**Title** is now \"Lighter and Heavier Implements for Speed-Strength: Choose a Mass That Keeps Technique, Size the Heavy One per Athlete, and Transfer Can Run One Way\" (see Disagreements 1).<br>**When to apply:** \"kept close to the competition mass, alternated with the normal implement, with the heavy option sized per athlete by whether technique holds\" is now \"chosen so technique holds, alternated with the normal implement, with the heavy option sized per athlete\". The Bondarchuk quote is now introduced as \"Inference: Bondarchuk goes further and argues for small deviations from competition weight; his correlation data: ...\".<br>**When NOT:** added \"Inference: several of the book's own examples are far from competition mass (a 3 kg javelin training weight; a 0.6-0.8 kg puck; 2 kg bats), so the page does not support a \"stay close to competition mass\" rule; only the shot and baseball data fit it, and the water polo result fits only in rank order (both medicine balls are far heavier than a match ball, and the lighter one did better).\"<br>**Related:** the Bondarchuk annotation \"(agrees: close to competition weight transfers best)\" is now \"(Bondarchuk's position: close to competition weight transfers best; this page does not test that)\". The old annotation contradicted the corrected body. |\n| heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix.md | **What it is (gap 2):** added the section's closing summary: \"The section closes on two levers: \"Perfecting the methods of developing speed-strength is a result of the search for a specific combination of means that exploit\" \"the positive after-effect of preceding work on the subsequent activity\" and \"the use of exercises in which muscular force opposes the inertia of the load and not its weight\" (PDF p. 278 / printed p. 267, verified against the page image). The after-effect lever is the arm-throw experiment in [[case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work]].\" The arm-throw case was not linked from this entry before.<br>**Related:** the implement-mass link now points to the new slug. |\n| _scope_supertraining_ch5a.txt | Lines 10 and 16 now list the two new filenames. Still 19 entries. |\n\n## Renames and inbound links\n\n| old slug | new slug | inbound links updated |\n|---|---|---|\n| claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves | claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves | `framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric.md` (Related) |\n| claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-stay-close-to-competition-mass | claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete | `heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix.md` (Related) |\n\nBefore the renames I grepped all of `staging/library`, `/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library` and the 5a/5b/5c scope files for both old slugs. The only hits were the two links above and the 5a scope file. No vault file links either old slug, and no 5b or 5c file does either. The review file and this log still mention the old slugs by design.\n\n## Disagreements with the review\n\n1. **Implement-mass title: I kept \"Transfer Can Run One Way\" instead of the review's \"Expect One-Way Transfer\".** The page supports one-way transfer only in two places. Egstrom is about precision, not speed (\"throwing the light ball improves precision for throwing the heavier ball, but the reverse effect does not occur\"). The shot transfer is \"apparently ... possible only by alternating\" (PDF 276-277 / p.265-266, image). \"Expect\" would overstate both, so the existing, weaker clause stays. The rest of the review's title is applied.\n2. **Implement mass: \"only the shot and baseball data fit it\" is refined, not copied.** Water polo also fits the closer-is-better direction by rank: the 2 kg ball beat the 4 kg ball, and 4 kg harmed technique (PDF 276 / p.265, image). It does not fit a \"stay close\" rule, because both balls are far heavier than a match ball. The added Inference says so. The match-ball comparison is outside-book knowledge, so it sits inside that Inference.\n3. **Newton II: the review's wording is tightened in three places.** The physics agrees with the review; the changes make it exact.\n   - Its (e) reads \"in general impulse = m(v_final - v_initial)\". Bare \"impulse\" repeats the looseness the review flagged in (c), so I wrote \"the impulse of the net external force equals m(v_final - v_initial)\".\n   - Its \"net propulsive impulse\" fixes take-off velocity only if the propulsive phase starts at zero vertical velocity, so I added \"which starts at zero vertical velocity at the bottom of the countermovement\".\n   - Its projectile example now carries \"(ignoring air resistance)\".\n   I also made two small changes the review did not list. The scope sentence now says its Δv is the change in velocity, not the book's ΔV, so it does not appear to contradict (e). The Why it works jump sentence is limited to \"a jump from a still start\".\n4. **Berger: one qualifier added.** Conclusion 5 (\"Training twice a week with 3 sets of the 10RM is as effective as training with 3 sets of 10RM three times a week\", PDF 271 / p.260) traces to the Berger 1963 frequency sentence. That sentence's regime is \"10RM for one set three times per week\" (PDF 270 / p.259). The entry now says that conclusion 5 speaks of 3 sets where the page describes one.\n5. **Three methods: the clash explanation carries \"Inference:\".** The review's replacement sentence (\"six reps cannot be done at 90% or more if five reps is 89%\") is reasoning, not the authors' statement, so it is labelled.\n\nNo review claim was found wrong on the page. Every flagged problem was confirmed on the rendered image before it was changed.\n\n## Proof\n\nGrep for the new wording, the old wording and the old slugs, run from `staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky`:\n\n```\n== Newton II\nwith Δv the change in velocity, not the book's ΔV\nfor a jump from a still start\nnet propulsive impulse (vertical ground reaction force minus body weight, integrated over the propulsive phase, which starts at zero vertical velocity at the bottom of the countermovement)\nFive sentences in the passage\nnet external force on the body.\nA projectile under gravity (ignoring air resistance) changes direction continuously while the force on it stays constant, so a change in velocity direction does not require a change in force, only a net force.\nin general the impulse of the net external force equals m(v_final - v_initial)\nDo not use (c), (d) or (e) as stated\n== Newton old wording (expect none)\n0\n== Dynamic correspondence\nThe paragraph that closes 5.1's dynamics discussion, after Fig 5.1\nInference: this passage is the authors' own statement\n== Three methods\nEric's baseline: off-season strength work prioritizes\nline 105, Inferred, transcript and shared slides\nInference: the 90-95% with 3-6 reps pairing clearly clashes with 5RM = 89% (six reps cannot be done at 90% or more if five reps is 89%)\n[[claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves]]\n== DeLorme\nthe book gives no subject populations for them. Inference: the DeLorme lineage began in rehabilitation, so untrained or patient samples are likely, but the page does not say so.\n== Berger\nmost weightlifting training is done with 1-3 repetitions for advanced weightlifters\ndrawn partly from data outside the five-programme list\nwhere that sentence describes one set\nTreat conclusion 6 as reported without its data\n== Ivanov\n# Ivanov (1966): Isometric Training Raised Isometric Strength Most, Eccentric Training Raised the Squat 1RM Most, and Only Concentric Training Raised the Vertical Jump\nInference: read as each mode training the nervous system for that mode; the book does not say this.\n== Implement mass\n# Lighter and Heavier Implements for Speed-Strength: Choose a Mass That Keeps Technique, Size the Heavy One per Athlete, and Transfer Can Run One Way\nchosen so technique holds, alternated with the normal implement, with the heavy option sized per athlete\nInference: Bondarchuk goes further and argues for small deviations from competition weight\nso the page does not support a \"stay close to competition mass\" rule\nBondarchuk's position: close to competition weight transfers best; this page does not test that\n== Loading\nThe section closes on two levers\n[[case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work]]\n[[claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete]]\n== Old slugs in staging/library, scope files, vault (expect nothing)\n(exit 1)\n== New files on disk\nclaim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete.md\nclaim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves.md\n== em dashes outside section headers in edited files (expect none)\n(end)\n== wrapped wikilinks (expect none)\n(end)\n```\n\nThe old-slug grep covered `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library`, the 5a, 5b and 5c scope files, and `/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library`. Exit 1 means no match.\n\nLint (Mac-path copy), run from the Flint root:\n\n```\n$ ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 114, 115]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1\n```\n\nThe one issue is the known `_book.md` false positive (title and table dashes). It is outside this fix scope; `_book.md` was not touched.\n\nFor the promoter: confidence tallies are unchanged. The Newton entry stays `proven`, scoped to the identity. The two renamed entries keep `supported`. The review's optional reciprocal-link suggestions were not applied, because they touch GO entries and 5b/5c files outside this fix scope.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":239,"cache_read_input_tokens":153890,"output_tokens":6460,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":46},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":239},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":6460,"cache_read_input_tokens":153890,"cache_creation_input_tokens":239,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":239,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_013oJc73ttWUwXXN7CYdwXNx":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch5a_applied.md","content":"# Fixes applied: Supertraining 5.1-5.2.3 (Ch5a), 19 entries\n\nFixer: Claude Opus. Date: 2026-10-03. Review: `staging/review_supertraining_ch5a_opus.md` (round 1, GO AFTER FIXES). The review's header counts \"FIX 8\", but its table has 7 FIX rows. Item 8 of its fix list is the inbound-link update after the two renames, not an eighth entry. All 7 rows, item 8, and the two \"Missing\" gaps were handled.\nSource checked: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`. I extracted the text of PDF idx 266-278 with PyMuPDF and rendered them to `staging/_renders/f5a_p<N>.png`. I opened the images for PDF 267 (Newton II, ΔV definition, Fig 5.1, neuromuscular paragraph), 268 (dynamic correspondence), 269 (5RM 89%, DeLorme), 270 (variants, Berger, 1-3 reps sentence), 271 (Berger conclusions 4-6, 85-95% = 3-5RM), 272 (Ivanov, 90-95% / 3-6), 276-277 (implements, Table 5.3) and 278 (two-lever summary). Page key: PDF idx = printed + 11.\nScope: the 19 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch5a.txt`. Only the 7 flagged entries were edited, plus the speed-strength loading entry (inbound link and gap 2). `_book.md` was not touched. No vault file was edited.\n\n| file | what changed |\n|---|---|\n| claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve.md | **What it is:** the `proven` scope sentence now reads \"(∫F_net dt = mΔv, with Δv the change in velocity, not the book's ΔV)\". Scope unchanged: the identity only.<br>**Why it works:** \"the take-off velocity of a jump fixes the net impulse\" is now \"for a jump from a still start, the take-off velocity fixes the net impulse\".<br>**When to apply:** \"propulsive impulse\" is now \"net propulsive impulse (vertical ground reaction force minus body weight, integrated over the propulsive phase, which starts at zero vertical velocity at the bottom of the countermovement)\".<br>**When NOT:** \"Four sentences\" is now \"Five sentences\". In (c), \"net force on a free body\" is now \"net external force on the body\". In (d), the circular-motion example is replaced: \"any change in the velocity vector, direction alone included, requires a net force, but the force itself need not change. A projectile under gravity (ignoring air resistance) changes direction continuously while the force on it stays constant, so a change in velocity direction does not require a change in force, only a net force.\" New (e) quotes the book's ΔV definition, \"The final velocity reached at the end of this phase\", and the 0-to-ΔV integral (PDF 267 / p.256, image). Then: \"Inference: so m.ΔV equals the change in momentum only when the body starts from rest; in general the impulse of the net external force equals m(v_final - v_initial).\" The closing line is now \"Do not use (c), (d) or (e) as stated.\" `confidence: proven` kept. |\n| heuristic--dynamic-correspondence-means-matching-motor-characteristics-not-copying-the-sport-movement.md | **What it is:** \"The paragraph before 5.1's dynamics discussion\" is now \"The paragraph that closes 5.1's dynamics discussion, after Fig 5.1\". The image confirms it: the neuromuscular paragraph follows Fig 5.1 at the foot of PDF 267, and \"All subsequent training ...\" opens PDF 268.<br>**When to apply:** \"This passage is the authors' own statement ...\" is now \"Inference: this passage is the authors' own statement ...\". |\n| framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric.md | **When NOT (4):** \"These three are not consistent; do not use any one as a rep-to-load conversion.\" is replaced. New text: \"Inference: the 90-95% with 3-6 reps pairing clearly clashes with 5RM = 89% (six reps cannot be done at 90% or more if five reps is 89%); the 85-95% = 3-5RM pairing is only loosely off (it implies a 5RM near 85%). Do not use any of them as a rep-to-load conversion.\"<br>**When to apply:** the sentence fragment is rewritten: \"Eric's baseline: off-season strength work prioritizes \"relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work\" (line 105, Inferred, transcript and shared slides).\" The baseline's own tag is \"Inferred (transcript + shared slides)\".<br>**Related:** the Ivanov link now points to the new slug. |\n| claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared.md | **When NOT:** \"All the studies are 1945-1960 work, mostly in rehabilitation or untrained subjects.\" is now \"All the studies are 1945-1960 work; the book gives no subject populations for them. Inference: the DeLorme lineage began in rehabilitation, so untrained or patient samples are likely, but the page does not say so.\" |\n| claim--berger-sets-reps-and-frequency-studies-in-novices.md | **When NOT:** the traceability sentence is replaced. New text: \"The six conclusions are drawn partly from data outside the five-programme list: conclusion 1's \"maximal weights three times per week\" and conclusion 5 trace to the Berger (1963) sentences (...PDF p. 270 / printed p. 259), though conclusion 5 speaks of \"3 sets of the 10RM\" where that sentence describes one set; conclusion 3 traces loosely to the 2RM/5RM/10RM and 3 x 6RM results; and conclusion 6 traces to no data on the page. Treat conclusion 6 as reported without its data.\"<br>**What it is (gap 1):** added after the Vorobyev (1971) clause: \"and \"Other studies\" show that \"most weightlifting training is done with 1-3 repetitions for advanced weightlifters, thereby emphasizing the importance of 1RM, 2RM and 3RM loads for enhancing competitive strength, especially where strength-skill is involved\" (PDF p. 270 / printed p. 259, verified against the page image; Vorobyev 1978 at PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260)\". The old combined locator \"PDF p. 270-271\" was split so each quote has its page. |\n| claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves.md (was `claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves.md`) | **Renamed** with `mv`.<br>**Title** is now \"Ivanov (1966): Isometric Training Raised Isometric Strength Most, Eccentric Training Raised the Squat 1RM Most, and Only Concentric Training Raised the Vertical Jump\". Checked against PDF 272 / p.261 (image): squat 1RM eccentric 15 kg (largest), isometric strength isometric 30.2 kg (largest), vertical jump +3.7 cm with concentric training only. There is no eccentric test.<br>**Why it works:** the gloss is moved out of the authors' voice. New text: \"\"the neuromuscular specificity of strength\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). They give no further mechanism. Inference: read as each mode training the nervous system for that mode; the book does not say this.\" |\n| claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete.md (was `claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-stay-close-to-competition-mass.md`) | **Renamed** with `mv`.<br>**Title** is now \"Lighter and Heavier Implements for Speed-Strength: Choose a Mass That Keeps Technique, Size the Heavy One per Athlete, and Transfer Can Run One Way\" (see Disagreements 1).<br>**When to apply:** \"kept close to the competition mass, alternated with the normal implement, with the heavy option sized per athlete by whether technique holds\" is now \"chosen so technique holds, alternated with the normal implement, with the heavy option sized per athlete\". The Bondarchuk quote is now introduced as \"Inference: Bondarchuk goes further and argues for small deviations from competition weight; his correlation data: ...\".<br>**When NOT:** added \"Inference: several of the book's own examples are far from competition mass (a 3 kg javelin training weight; a 0.6-0.8 kg puck; 2 kg bats), so the page does not support a \"stay close to competition mass\" rule; only the shot and baseball data fit it, and the water polo result fits only in rank order (both medicine balls are far heavier than a match ball, and the lighter one did better).\"<br>**Related:** the Bondarchuk annotation \"(agrees: close to competition weight transfers best)\" is now \"(Bondarchuk's position: close to competition weight transfers best; this page does not test that)\". The old annotation contradicted the corrected body. |\n| heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix.md | **What it is (gap 2):** added the section's closing summary: \"The section closes on two levers: \"Perfecting the methods of developing speed-strength is a result of the search for a specific combination of means that exploit\" \"the positive after-effect of preceding work on the subsequent activity\" and \"the use of exercises in which muscular force opposes the inertia of the load and not its weight\" (PDF p. 278 / printed p. 267, verified against the page image). The after-effect lever is the arm-throw experiment in [[case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work]].\" The arm-throw case was not linked from this entry before.<br>**Related:** the implement-mass link now points to the new slug. |\n| _scope_supertraining_ch5a.txt | Lines 10 and 16 now list the two new filenames. Still 19 entries. |\n\n## Renames and inbound links\n\n| old slug | new slug | inbound links updated |\n|---|---|---|\n| claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves | claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves | `framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric.md` (Related) |\n| claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-stay-close-to-competition-mass | claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete | `heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix.md` (Related) |\n\nBefore the renames I grepped all of `staging/library`, `/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library` and the 5a/5b/5c scope files for both old slugs. The only hits were the two links above and the 5a scope file. No vault file links either old slug, and no 5b or 5c file does either. The review file and this log still mention the old slugs by design.\n\n## Disagreements with the review\n\n1. **Implement-mass title: I kept \"Transfer Can Run One Way\" instead of the review's \"Expect One-Way Transfer\".** The page supports one-way transfer only in two places. Egstrom is about precision, not speed (\"throwing the light ball improves precision for throwing the heavier ball, but the reverse effect does not occur\"). The shot transfer is \"apparently ... possible only by alternating\" (PDF 276-277 / p.265-266, image). \"Expect\" would overstate both, so the existing, weaker clause stays. The rest of the review's title is applied.\n2. **Implement mass: \"only the shot and baseball data fit it\" is refined, not copied.** Water polo also fits the closer-is-better direction by rank: the 2 kg ball beat the 4 kg ball, and 4 kg harmed technique (PDF 276 / p.265, image). It does not fit a \"stay close\" rule, because both balls are far heavier than a match ball. The added Inference says so. The match-ball comparison is outside-book knowledge, so it sits inside that Inference.\n3. **Newton II: the review's wording is tightened in three places.** The physics agrees with the review; the changes make it exact.\n   - Its (e) reads \"in general impulse = m(v_final - v_initial)\". Bare \"impulse\" repeats the looseness the review flagged in (c), so I wrote \"the impulse of the net external force equals m(v_final - v_initial)\".\n   - Its \"net propulsive impulse\" fixes take-off velocity only if the propulsive phase starts at zero vertical velocity, so I added \"which starts at zero vertical velocity at the bottom of the countermovement\".\n   - Its projectile example now carries \"(ignoring air resistance)\".\n   I also made two small changes the review did not list. The scope sentence now says its Δv is the change in velocity, not the book's ΔV, so it does not appear to contradict (e). The Why it works jump sentence is limited to \"a jump from a still start\".\n4. **Berger: one qualifier added.** Conclusion 5 (\"Training twice a week with 3 sets of the 10RM is as effective as training with 3 sets of 10RM three times a week\", PDF 271 / p.260) traces to the Berger 1963 frequency sentence. That sentence's regime is \"10RM for one set three times per week\" (PDF 270 / p.259). The entry now says that conclusion 5 speaks of 3 sets where the page describes one.\n5. **Three methods: the clash explanation carries \"Inference:\".** The review's replacement sentence (\"six reps cannot be done at 90% or more if five reps is 89%\") is reasoning, not the authors' statement, so it is labelled.\n\nNo review claim was found wrong on the page. Every flagged problem was confirmed on the rendered image before it was changed.\n\n## Proof\n\nGrep for the new wording, the old wording and the old slugs, run from `staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky`:\n\n```\n== Newton II\nwith Δv the change in velocity, not the book's ΔV\nfor a jump from a still start\nnet propulsive impulse (vertical ground reaction force minus body weight, integrated over the propulsive phase, which starts at zero vertical velocity at the bottom of the countermovement)\nFive sentences in the passage\nnet external force on the body.\nA projectile under gravity (ignoring air resistance) changes direction continuously while the force on it stays constant, so a change in velocity direction does not require a change in force, only a net force.\nin general the impulse of the net external force equals m(v_final - v_initial)\nDo not use (c), (d) or (e) as stated\n== Newton old wording (expect none)\n0\n== Dynamic correspondence\nThe paragraph that closes 5.1's dynamics discussion, after Fig 5.1\nInference: this passage is the authors' own statement\n== Three methods\nEric's baseline: off-season strength work prioritizes\nline 105, Inferred, transcript and shared slides\nInference: the 90-95% with 3-6 reps pairing clearly clashes with 5RM = 89% (six reps cannot be done at 90% or more if five reps is 89%)\n[[claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves]]\n== DeLorme\nthe book gives no subject populations for them. Inference: the DeLorme lineage began in rehabilitation, so untrained or patient samples are likely, but the page does not say so.\n== Berger\nmost weightlifting training is done with 1-3 repetitions for advanced weightlifters\ndrawn partly from data outside the five-programme list\nwhere that sentence describes one set\nTreat conclusion 6 as reported without its data\n== Ivanov\n# Ivanov (1966): Isometric Training Raised Isometric Strength Most, Eccentric Training Raised the Squat 1RM Most, and Only Concentric Training Raised the Vertical Jump\nInference: read as each mode training the nervous system for that mode; the book does not say this.\n== Implement mass\n# Lighter and Heavier Implements for Speed-Strength: Choose a Mass That Keeps Technique, Size the Heavy One per Athlete, and Transfer Can Run One Way\nchosen so technique holds, alternated with the normal implement, with the heavy option sized per athlete\nInference: Bondarchuk goes further and argues for small deviations from competition weight\nso the page does not support a \"stay close to competition mass\" rule\nBondarchuk's position: close to competition weight transfers best; this page does not test that\n== Loading\nThe section closes on two levers\n[[case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work]]\n[[claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete]]\n== Old slugs in staging/library, scope files, vault (expect nothing)\n(exit 1)\n== New files on disk\nclaim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete.md\nclaim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-