{"parentUuid":"18ff286f-7797-43e8-8d00-a5ab188da8e8","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"03e4fd49-e0c7-4b5d-bb77-f25bc8e8f726","agentId":"ad490fb3266cc50f1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FVD7cGmh6uHm6qXfhgsjrM","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch5a_applied.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"608c74e4-a65a-4e2c-89e5-788b91d67f61","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:19:53.438Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch5a_applied.md","oldString":"The old combined locator \"PDF p. 270-271\" was split so each quote has its page.","newString":"The paragraph's closing locator, which used to read \"PDF p. 270-271 / printed p. 259-260\", now gives a page for each quote it covers. The opening locator of What it is (\"PDF p. 270-271 / printed p. 259-260\") is unchanged.","originalFile":null,"structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":10,"oldLines":7,"newStart":10,"newLines":7,"lines":[" | heuristic--dynamic-correspondence-means-matching-motor-characteristics-not-copying-the-sport-movement.md | **What it is:** \"The paragraph before 5.1's dynamics discussion\" is now \"The paragraph that closes 5.1's dynamics discussion, after Fig 5.1\". The image confirms it: the neuromuscular paragraph follows Fig 5.1 at the foot of PDF 267, and \"All subsequent training ...\" opens PDF 268.<br>**When to apply:** \"This passage is the authors' own statement ...\" is now \"Inference: this passage is the authors' own statement ...\". |"," | framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric.md | **When NOT (4):** \"These three are not consistent; do not use any one as a rep-to-load conversion.\" is replaced. New text: \"Inference: the 90-95% with 3-6 reps pairing clearly clashes with 5RM = 89% (six reps cannot be done at 90% or more if five reps is 89%); the 85-95% = 3-5RM pairing is only loosely off (it implies a 5RM near 85%). Do not use any of them as a rep-to-load conversion.\"<br>**When to apply:** the sentence fragment is rewritten: \"Eric's baseline: off-season strength work prioritizes \"relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work\" (line 105, Inferred, transcript and shared slides).\" The baseline's own tag is \"Inferred (transcript + shared slides)\".<br>**Related:** the Ivanov link now points to the new slug. |"," | claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared.md | **When NOT:** \"All the studies are 1945-1960 work, mostly in rehabilitation or untrained subjects.\" is now \"All the studies are 1945-1960 work; the book gives no subject populations for them. Inference: the DeLorme lineage began in rehabilitation, so untrained or patient samples are likely, but the page does not say so.\" |","-| claim--berger-sets-reps-and-frequency-studies-in-novices.md | **When NOT:** the traceability sentence is replaced. New text: \"The six conclusions are drawn partly from data outside the five-programme list: conclusion 1's \"maximal weights three times per week\" and conclusion 5 trace to the Berger (1963) sentences (...PDF p. 270 / printed p. 259), though conclusion 5 speaks of \"3 sets of the 10RM\" where that sentence describes one set; conclusion 3 traces loosely to the 2RM/5RM/10RM and 3 x 6RM results; and conclusion 6 traces to no data on the page. Treat conclusion 6 as reported without its data.\"<br>**What it is (gap 1):** added after the Vorobyev (1971) clause: \"and \"Other studies\" show that \"most weightlifting training is done with 1-3 repetitions for advanced weightlifters, thereby emphasizing the importance of 1RM, 2RM and 3RM loads for enhancing competitive strength, especially where strength-skill is involved\" (PDF p. 270 / printed p. 259, verified against the page image; Vorobyev 1978 at PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260)\". The old combined locator \"PDF p. 270-271\" was split so each quote has its page. |","+| claim--berger-sets-reps-and-frequency-studies-in-novices.md | **When NOT:** the traceability sentence is replaced. New text: \"The six conclusions are drawn partly from data outside the five-programme list: conclusion 1's \"maximal weights three times per week\" and conclusion 5 trace to the Berger (1963) sentences (...PDF p. 270 / printed p. 259), though conclusion 5 speaks of \"3 sets of the 10RM\" where that sentence describes one set; conclusion 3 traces loosely to the 2RM/5RM/10RM and 3 x 6RM results; and conclusion 6 traces to no data on the page. Treat conclusion 6 as reported without its data.\"<br>**What it is (gap 1):** added after the Vorobyev (1971) clause: \"and \"Other studies\" show that \"most weightlifting training is done with 1-3 repetitions for advanced weightlifters, thereby emphasizing the importance of 1RM, 2RM and 3RM loads for enhancing competitive strength, especially where strength-skill is involved\" (PDF p. 270 / printed p. 259, verified against the page image; Vorobyev 1978 at PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260)\". The paragraph's closing locator, which used to read \"PDF p. 270-271 / printed p. 259-260\", now gives a page for each quote it covers. The opening locator of What it is (\"PDF p. 270-271 / printed p. 259-260\") is unchanged. |"," | claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves.md (was `claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves.md`) | **Renamed** with `mv`.<br>**Title** is now \"Ivanov (1966): Isometric Training Raised Isometric Strength Most, Eccentric Training Raised the Squat 1RM Most, and Only Concentric Training Raised the Vertical Jump\". Checked against PDF 272 / p.261 (image): squat 1RM eccentric 15 kg (largest), isometric strength isometric 30.2 kg (largest), vertical jump +3.7 cm with concentric training only. There is no eccentric test.<br>**Why it works:** the gloss is moved out of the authors' voice. New text: \"\"the neuromuscular specificity of strength\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). They give no further mechanism. Inference: read as each mode training the nervous system for that mode; the book does not say this.\" |"," | claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete.md (was `claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-stay-close-to-competition-mass.md`) | **Renamed** with `mv`.<br>**Title** is now \"Lighter and Heavier Implements for Speed-Strength: Choose a Mass That Keeps Technique, Size the Heavy One per Athlete, and Transfer Can Run One Way\" (see Disagreements 1).<br>**When to apply:** \"kept close to the competition mass, alternated with the normal implement, with the heavy option sized per athlete by whether technique holds\" is now \"chosen so technique holds, alternated with the normal implement, with the heavy option sized per athlete\". The Bondarchuk quote is now introduced as \"Inference: Bondarchuk goes further and argues for small deviations from competition weight; his correlation data: ...\".<br>**When NOT:** added \"Inference: several of the book's own examples are far from competition mass (a 3 kg javelin training weight; a 0.6-0.8 kg puck; 2 kg bats), so the page does not support a \"stay close to competition mass\" rule; only the shot and baseball data fit it, and the water polo result fits only in rank order (both medicine balls are far heavier than a match ball, and the lighter one did better).\"<br>**Related:** the Bondarchuk annotation \"(agrees: close to competition weight transfers best)\" is now \"(Bondarchuk's position: close to competition weight transfers best; this page does not test that)\". The old annotation contradicted the corrected body. |"," | heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix.md | **What it is (gap 2):** added the section's closing summary: \"The section closes on two levers: \"Perfecting the methods of developing speed-strength is a result of the search for a specific combination of means that exploit\" \"the positive after-effect of preceding work on the subsequent activity\" and \"the use of exercises in which muscular force opposes the inertia of the load and not its weight\" (PDF p. 278 / printed p. 267, verified against the page image). The after-effect lever is the arm-throw experiment in [[case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work]].\" The arm-throw case was not linked from this entry before.<br>**Related:** the implement-mass link now points to the new slug. |"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"18ff286f-7797-43e8-8d00-a5ab188da8e8","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
