{"parentUuid":"51d7b8cb-0954-415b-9338-4cbf6a22f333","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ad490fb3266cc50f1","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffcE7imtRqtQxBpX7A71","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I applied every fix in the review: all 7 FIX rows, item 8 (the inbound links after the renames) and both coverage gaps. I checked each claim against the rendered page images (PDF 267-272 and 276-278) before changing anything, and none of the review's claims turned out wrong. In a few places I tightened its wording; those are listed under \"Disagreements with the review\" in the log at `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch5a_applied.md`.\n\nThe review's header says \"FIX 8\", but its table has only 7 FIX rows. Its item 8 is the link update, not an eighth entry; the log says so.\n\n## Per-fix outcome\n1. **Newton II: applied.** ΔV is now flagged as the book's final velocity, with the integral running from 0. \"Propulsive impulse\" became \"net propulsive impulse\", \"net force on a free body\" became \"net external force on the body\", and the circular-motion example became the constant-gravity projectile. \"Four sentences\" is now \"Five\". `proven` stays, scoped to the identity only. My tightenings, with the physics labelled Inference:\n   - New point (e) says \"the impulse of the **net external force** equals m(v_final - v_initial)\", not bare \"impulse\".\n   - Net propulsive impulse is defined over a phase that starts at zero vertical velocity at the bottom of the countermovement.\n   - The projectile example adds \"ignoring air resistance\".\n   - The scope sentence now says its Δv is the change in velocity, not the book's ΔV.\n   - The Why it works jump sentence is limited to a jump from a still start.\n2. **Dynamic correspondence: applied.** The paragraph is now located as the one that closes the 5.1 dynamics discussion, after Fig 5.1. \"Inference:\" now prefixes the line saying this passage is the authors' own statement of the rule.\n3. **Three methods: applied.** The text now names which load-rep pairing clashes, labelled Inference: the 90-95% with 3-6 reps pairing clearly clashes with 5RM = 89%, and 85-95% = 3-5RM is only loosely off. The line-105 fragment is now a proper \"Eric's baseline:\" sentence.\n4. **DeLorme: applied.** The entry now says the book gives no subject populations. The rehabilitation point is kept only as a labelled Inference.\n5. **Berger: applied.** The traceability sentence is corrected: only conclusion 6 has no data on the page. I added a qualifier that conclusion 5 says 3 sets of the 10RM where Berger 1963 describes one. Gap 1 is folded in: the 1-3 rep sentence for advanced lifters, PDF 270 / p.259.\n6. **Ivanov: applied.** New title and rename. The \"trains the nervous system for that mode\" gloss is now labelled Inference.\n7. **Implement mass: applied, with two refinements.**\n   - I kept \"Transfer Can Run One Way\" rather than the review's \"Expect One-Way Transfer\". Egstrom's result is about precision only, and the shot transfer is \"apparently ... possible only by alternating\", so \"Expect\" says more than the page does.\n   - The Inference on counterexamples also notes that water polo fits \"closer is better\" by rank only.\n   - The Bondarchuk note in Related no longer says \"agrees\"; it now attributes the view to Bondarchuk.\n8. **Inbound links: applied.**\n- **Gap 2: applied.** The two-lever summary from PDF 278 / p.267 is now in the speed-strength loading entry's What it is, with a link to the arm-throw case.\n\n## Renames and inbound links\n\n| old slug | new slug | link updated in |\n|---|---|---|\n| `claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves` | `claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves` | three-methods entry, Related |\n| `claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-stay-close-to-competition-mass` | `claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete` | speed-strength loading entry, Related |\n\n- Lines 10 and 16 of `_scope_supertraining_ch5a.txt` now list the new filenames.\n- I searched the vault and the 5b and 5c files for both old slugs; none of them link to either, so there's nothing in the vault to report.\n\n## Grep proof\n```\n== Newton II\nwith Δv the change in velocity, not the book's ΔV\nfor a jump from a still start\nnet propulsive impulse (vertical ground reaction force minus body weight, integrated over the propulsive phase, which starts at zero vertical velocity at the bottom of the countermovement)\nFive sentences in the passage\nnet external force on the body.\nA projectile under gravity (ignoring air resistance) changes direction continuously while the force on it stays constant, so a change in velocity direction does not require a change in force, only a net force.\nin general the impulse of the net external force equals m(v_final - v_initial)\nDo not use (c), (d) or (e) as stated\n== Newton old wording (expect none)\n0\n== Dynamic correspondence\nThe paragraph that closes 5.1's dynamics discussion, after Fig 5.1\nInference: this passage is the authors' own statement\n== Three methods\nEric's baseline: off-season strength work prioritizes\nline 105, Inferred, transcript and shared slides\nInference: the 90-95% with 3-6 reps pairing clearly clashes with 5RM = 89% (six reps cannot be done at 90% or more if five reps is 89%)\n[[claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves]]\n== DeLorme\nthe book gives no subject populations for them. Inference: the DeLorme lineage began in rehabilitation, so untrained or patient samples are likely, but the page does not say so.\n== Berger\nmost weightlifting training is done with 1-3 repetitions for advanced weightlifters\ndrawn partly from data outside the five-programme list\nwhere that sentence describes one set\nTreat conclusion 6 as reported without its data\n== Ivanov\n# Ivanov (1966): Isometric Training Raised Isometric Strength Most, Eccentric Training Raised the Squat 1RM Most, and Only Concentric Training Raised the Vertical Jump\nInference: read as each mode training the nervous system for that mode; the book does not say this.\n== Implement mass\n# Lighter and Heavier Implements for Speed-Strength: Choose a Mass That Keeps Technique, Size the Heavy One per Athlete, and Transfer Can Run One Way\nchosen so technique holds, alternated with the normal implement, with the heavy option sized per athlete\nInference: Bondarchuk goes further and argues for small deviations from competition weight\nso the page does not support a \"stay close to competition mass\" rule\nBondarchuk's position: close to competition weight transfers best; this page does not test that\n== Loading\nThe section closes on two levers\n[[case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work]]\n[[claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete]]\n== Old slugs in staging/library, scope files, vault (expect nothing)\n(exit 1)\n== New files on disk\nclaim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete.md\nclaim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves.md\n== em dashes outside section headers in edited files (expect none)\n(end)\n== wrapped wikilinks (expect none)\n(end)\n```\nFor the old-slug search, \"exit 1\" means no match anywhere.\n\n## Lint\n```\n$ ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 114, 115]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1\n```\nThe one issue is the known false positive in `_book.md`, which I didn't touch.\n\nNo git, no vault edits. I didn't apply the review's optional reciprocal-link suggestions, because they would mean editing entries that passed review and files in 5b and 5c, which are outside this fix pass."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details"